---
/*
 * cbf-lib-cost-table — the vendor Line Item Budget (LIB) entry grid.
 * The LIB tab of a CBFish contract (/lib-entry).
 *
 * ── THE DESIGN CLAIM ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A LIB is a WEB OF CROSS-LINE DEPENDENCIES, not a list of independent rows:
 * fringe is a percentage of a salary line, a cell-phone line's months are LINKED
 * to a position's staffing months, indirect is a percentage of the sum of
 * whatever the rate is scoped to. A grid can hold that web — you can see the
 * salary line and everything that follows it at once. A one-line-at-a-time modal
 * fights it. So THERE IS NO ADD-LINE MODAL HERE: "Add line" appends an editable
 * row inline, of the shape the vendor picked, focused and ready to type.
 *
 * ── EVERY CATEGORY CAPTIONS ITS OWN COLUMNS ─────────────────────────────────
 * There is no global <thead>. One header for nine categories forced every
 * section to answer to the same three words — and "Quantity" means months in
 * Personnel, nights in a lodging line, units in Supplies, and nothing at all in
 * Fringe. So each category emits its own caption row under its heading, in its
 * own vocabulary (`columns` in src/data/lib-entry.mjs). Only the three columns
 * that DON'T move keep one name throughout — Amount, Work element and the row
 * action — because the money column has to line up with the sub-block subtotals,
 * the indirect base and the grand total in the table foot.
 *
 * ── THE FIVE LINE SHAPES — the ROW decides its cells, not the category ───────
 * Several categories mix shapes (Vehicles carries B and A; Supplies carries A
 * and D), so the shape is per-row and each row renders only the cells its shape
 * actually has. A collapsed cell is an em dash or a spanned cell — never a dead
 * input.
 *   A  qty-rate       label · qty (+ optional 2nd qty) · unit · cost per unit.
 *                     The vendor TYPES the rate. amount = qty × qty2 × rate.
 *   B  authority-rate label · GSA location + season · qty (+ 2nd qty). The rate
 *                     is REFERENCED from the GSA schedule, rendered as a value
 *                     with its source, not as an open input. A vendor may
 *                     override it; an override above `ceiling` is the failure.
 *   C  pct-of-base    a percentage of a base. NO qty, NO unit, and — in the one
 *                     category that uses it — NO input either: every shape-C row
 *                     here is GENERATED from a salary line. The row shows what it
 *                     is a percentage of, the resolved base dollars, and which
 *                     line to edit, so the dependency is legible from both ends.
 *   D  lump           label · one dollar amount. No qty, no rate.
 *   E  travel-line    belongs to a TRIP and picks a travel KIND. Nothing about
 *                     it is free text: the kind fixes the unit and the slice of
 *                     the schedule the rate may come from, and the trip supplies
 *                     the locality, the travelers and the occurrence count.
 *                     amount = occurrences × count × duration × rate.
 *
 * ── TRIPS, AND WHY TRAVEL IS THE ONE CATEGORY WITH ITS OWN SHAPE ─────────────
 * Travel is the most RULE-BOUND category in a LIB and was being entered as the
 * least structured: a generic "3 persons × 10 days" typed into two free-text
 * quantity boxes, against a schedule row nothing stopped you picking wrong. Now
 * a trip is a sub-block — the same machinery Supplies uses for Office/Field —
 * carrying destination, window and `occurrences`. That last
 * one is what the flat list could not say at all: a semiannual coordination
 * meeting is ONE trip that happens twice, not two rows a reviewer has to
 * recognise as the same journey. Change the trip and every line under it moves,
 * which is the same cross-line dependency the rest of this grid is built around.
 *
 * ── THE LINE'S IDENTITY IS A CONTROL, NOT A TEXTBOX ──────────────────────────
 * Four categories know what a line is called better than the vendor does, so
 * they replace the free-text description: Personnel picks a position off the
 * roster, Travel picks a kind, Vehicles picks a kind, and Fringe names itself
 * after the salary line it follows. All five identity variants ship on every row
 * and one is revealed — which is what keeps a CLONED row correct when it is
 * added to, or moved into, a different category.
 *
 * ── FRINGE IS TYPED ON THE SALARY ROW AND READ IN ITS OWN SECTION ────────────
 * Fringe is not a budgeting decision — it is an organization's rate applied to a
 * position. The vendor does not choose it, scope it, or split it across work
 * elements; it follows the salary. Authoring it as an independent line asked for
 * three gestures where there were no choices (create a line, point it at a base,
 * type an allocation), and every one of them was a way for it to drift from the
 * salary it is a consequence of — which is exactly what the seed data had done.
 *
 * So the RATE is a pre-filled field in the salary row's rate cell, with the
 * dollars it just generated stated under it, and Personnel — Fringe is a
 * READ-OUT: one row per position, no percentage input, no base picker, no
 * allocation editor, no delete, no add. The section stays, because that is the
 * object class a funder reads, a reviewer disputes by name, and the indirect
 * rate is scoped by (`indirectConfig.appliesTo` is a list of CATEGORIES). What
 * moved is where fringe is typed, not where it is read.
 *
 * `syncFringeLines()` owns the set — see the block comment on it in
 * src/data/lib-entry.mjs — and `syncFringeRows()` below reconciles the rows it
 * produces with the DOM, on the same recompute that prices them.
 *
 * ── ARITHMETIC IS NEVER DONE HERE ───────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every amount, subtotal, indirect base and total comes from
 * `priceLines(lines, cfg)` in src/data/lib-entry.mjs, which resolves the
 * dependency order (linked quantities → A/B/D → C → indirect). It is re-called
 * in full after EVERY edit, so a dependent line can never silently disagree with
 * its base. `dependentsOf(id)` names exactly the rows that just moved because of
 * an edit, and they get a brief tint — the money moment of the prototype.
 *
 * ── GATES, RENDERED IN BOTH STATES ON LOAD ──────────────────────────────────
 *   · gsa-over-ceiling — trv-4 Boise lodging overrides GSA at $172 against a
 *     $155 ceiling: danger badge + esa-field-error naming ceiling and overage.
 *   · matches-schedule — every other schedule-rated line renders the PASSING
 *     side of the same gate ("Matches GSA schedule"), so it is legible either way.
 *   · no-allocation — sal-4 Administrative Specialist reads "Not allocated" in
 *     the warning tone with an esa-field-error. Added rows start `allocations:{}`
 *     — correctly unallocated, which moves the gate the moment a line is added.
 *     Its FRINGE row reads unallocated too, and says so by naming the salary
 *     line: an inherited split cannot be fixed where it is inherited. That pair
 *     used to be able to disagree — the seed data shipped a fringe line fully
 *     allocated to work element A off a salary line allocated to nothing, and no
 *     check looked at the two together.
 *   · capitalization threshold — tested in BOTH directions and shown failing on
 *     load: sup-f4, a $6,200 trap assembly, is filed under Supplies and belongs
 *     in Capital Equipment. The failing row offers the MOVE, not just a verdict,
 *     because the item is correct — only its category is wrong, and retyping it
 *     elsewhere would lose its work-element split.
 *   · contract period — every salary line tests its months against the
 *     12-month period, passing on load and failing live past it.
 *
 * ── EVENT CONTRACT (all on `document`) ──────────────────────────────────────
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-lib:totals' — on mount and after EVERY recompute
 *   detail: { total, direct, indirect, indirectBase, remaining, pctOfValue,
 *             failing, unallocated, capitalFailing, monthsFailing }
 *   failing        = gsaFailingLines(lines).length
 *   unallocated    = unallocatedLines(lines).length
 *   capitalFailing = overrides + untestable lumps        // what the sort can't settle
 *   monthsFailing  = monthsOverPeriod(lines).length
 * DISPATCH 'cbf-lib:open-subcontract' — a subcontracts row's jump-out
 *   detail: { sheetId, lineId }        // sheetId = line.sheet
 * LISTEN   'cbf-lib:indirect-config' — a sibling owns the overhead block,
 *   including WHICH categories the rate applies to.
 *   detail: { config }                 // shape of indirectConfig
 *   On receipt: re-run priceLines(lines, config), repaint the indirect base,
 *   indirect and total (the base visibly moves as the scope changes), and
 *   re-fire 'cbf-lib:totals'.
 *
 * bcn-lego-checked: the house rule points data tables at AG Grid, but that rule
 * governs SCANNABLE data grids with search / filter / download chrome. This is an
 * ENTRY FORM — a live control and its own validation state in most cells, with
 * per-row shape branching and colspan collapse — so a semantic <table> carrying
 * esa-* controls is both correct and far lighter. Same call cbf-baselines-table
 * .astro and cbf-lib-pi-documents.astro already made in this spoke; the full
 * ecology catalog was re-checked and has no table/grid lego at all. Everything
 * that ISN'T the grid itself is a lego: entry cells = esa-text-field, GSA row and
 * percentage base = esa-select, add-line = esa-button / esa-dropdown-menu (shape
 * picker), allocation editor = esa-popover, row delete = esa-icon-button,
 * validation = esa-field-error, gate verdicts = esa-badge. Beacon is not cloned
 * on this machine. The row grab handle added for reordering is cbf-drag-handle
 * (itself an esa-icon-button) driven by the shared src/lib/row-reorder.ts — the
 * catalog has no sortable/drag lego, and writing the gesture per surface would
 * have given the prototype three different drag feels.
 *
 * ── ORDER IS A THING THE VENDOR OWNS ────────────────────────────────────────
 * Every non-derived row carries a grab handle: a budget is read top to bottom by
 * whoever approves it, and the order lines were TYPED in is rarely the order
 * they should be READ in. Dragging reorders within the category, and crossing a
 * sub-block boundary while doing it MOVES the line — Office → Field in Supplies,
 * one trip to another in Travel — because that is the only thing the gesture
 * could honestly mean once the row is under a different heading.
 *
 * Travel alone adds a third outcome: dropping a line onto the MIDDLE of another
 * travel line groups the two into a new trip. That gesture belongs to Travel
 * because a trip is the only sub-block on this screen that is a FACT the vendor
 * discovers rather than a filing drawer the form ships with — Office and Field
 * are fixed, positions are fixed, but "these two costs are actually the same
 * journey" is a realisation you have while looking at the rows. See onDrop().
 */
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import EsaBadge from '@esa/ecology/esa-badge.astro';
import EsaFieldError from '@esa/ecology/esa-field-error.astro';
import EsaIconButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import CbfDragHandle from '../shared/cbf-drag-handle.astro';
import '../../styles/cbf-row-reorder.css';
import {
  SHAPES,
  categories,
  categoryOf,
  costLines,
  priceLines,
  effectiveRate,
  effectiveQty,
  baseAmountFor,
  baseLabelFor,
  gsaRate,
  isScheduleRated,
  workElements,
  weLabel,
  contract,
  contractMonths,
  positionKey,
  rosterFor,
  PERSONNEL_TYPES,
  TIME_UNITS,
  timeUnit,
  isSalaryLine,
  timeInMonths,
  periodProblem,
  tripOf,
  NO_TRIP,
  isLooseTravel,
  TRAVEL_KINDS,
  travelKind,
  travelChain,
  travelCount,
  travelLabel,
  travelLocality,
  fringeLabel,
  fringePctOf,
  fringeOnLine,
  isDerivedFringe,
  DEFAULT_FRINGE_PCT,
  ENTRY_COLUMNS,
  hasFringeCell,
  rateSpan,
  VEHICLE_KINDS,
  vehicleKind,
  perItemCost,
  onThreshold,
  thresholdTested,
  thresholdTargetFor,
  isClassOverride,
  canOverrideThreshold,
  autoFileEquipment,
  blockOf,
  filingConsequence,
  countOf,
  indirectConfig,
  usd,
  pct,
  qtyFmt,
  equipmentClassLabel,
  personnelSplit,
  tripLabel,
} from '../../data/lib-entry.mjs';
import {
  SEVERITIES,
  severityMeta,
  reasonLabel,
  seedReview,
  flagFor,
  isIssued,
} from '../../data/lib-review.mjs';

interface Props {
  /** Section heading above the grid. Pass null to let the page own the heading. */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** One line of context under the heading. Pass null to omit. */
  lede?: string | null;
}
const {
  heading = 'Line item budget',
  lede = `${contract.number} · ${contract.fiscalYear} · ${contract.periodStart}–${contract.periodEnd}`,
} = Astro.props;

const lines: any[] = costLines;
const priced = priceLines(lines, indirectConfig);
const amt = (id: string): number => priced.amounts[id] ?? 0;

/* ── The COR's review layer ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Server-rendered from the seeded review, then kept live by the script. A mark
   belongs ON the line it is about, so it renders inside the cost-line cell —
   under the description it disputes — rather than in a list somewhere else. */
const review = seedReview();
const flagOf = (l: any) => flagFor(review, l.id);
const reviewIssued = isIssued(review);

/* The four shapes are a MODEL concept, not vendor-facing copy — a vendor never
   thinks "this is a shape-A line". The shape shows itself through which cells the
   row exposes; naming it on every row was internal vocabulary leaking into the UI
   (and it pushed the Rate-source control a line below its neighbours). Removed. */

/* ── View derivations ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Every helper below lives in src/lib/lib-entry-view.ts and is bound here to
   THIS scope's data. The bodies used to be written twice — once here for SSR and
   once in the client script block — which is how the client copy of qtyFactText lost
   its travel branch while the SSR copy kept it. Binding instead of re-declaring
   makes that class of drift impossible: there is one body, and the two scopes
   differ only in what they pass it. */
import * as V from '../../lib/lib-entry-view';

const A = priced.amounts;

const cents = V.cents;
const money = V.money;
const numText = V.numText;

const gsaOf = V.gsaOf;
const overridden = V.overridden;
const gsaOver = V.gsaOver;
const gsaSrcText = V.gsaSrcText;
const gsaErrText = V.gsaErrText;
const gsaLabel = V.gsaLabel;
/* Travel's rate cell — the claim, and what it resolves to. */
const claimsStandard = V.claimsStandard;
const canClaimStandard = V.canClaimStandard;
const standardSrcText = V.standardSrcText;
const typedSrcText = V.typedSrcText;
const standardAmountText = V.standardAmountText;
const standardBadgeText = V.standardBadgeText;

const perItem = (l: any) => V.perItem(l, A);
const capOverride = V.capOverride;
const capLumpOpen = (l: any) => V.capLumpOpen(l, A);
const capPass = (l: any) => V.capPass(l, A);
const capFiledText = (l: any) => V.capFiledText(l, A);
const capWhyText = (l: any) => V.capWhyText(l, indirectConfig);
const capOverrideText = (l: any) => V.capOverrideText(l, A);
const capLumpText = (l: any) => V.capLumpText(l, A);
const capCanOverride = (l: any) => V.capCanOverride(l, A);
const capToggleText = V.capToggleText;

const monthsTested = V.monthsTested;
const monthsOver = (l: any) => V.monthsOver(l, lines);
const monthsText = (l: any) => V.monthsText(l, lines);
const monthsErrText = (l: any) => V.monthsErrText(l, lines);
const rateLabelOf = V.rateLabelOf;
const rateSuffixOf = V.rateSuffixOf;

const fringePctText = V.fringePctText;
const fringeAmtText = (l: any) => V.fringeAmtText(l, lines, A);
const fringeFactText = V.fringeFactText;
const derivedRow = V.derivedRow;
const fringeSrcText = (l: any) => V.fringeSrcText(l, lines);
const fringeRateText = (l: any) => V.fringeRateText(l, lines);
const derivedAllocErrText = (l: any) => V.derivedAllocErrText(l, lines);

const identOf = V.identOf;
const displayLabel = (l: any) => V.displayLabel(l, lines);

const unitLocked = V.unitLocked;
const lockedUnit = V.lockedUnit;
const lockedUnit2 = V.lockedUnit2;
const unitPicked = V.unitPicked;
const unitStatic = V.unitStatic;

const allocEntries = V.allocEntries;
const allocSum = V.allocSum;
const allocSummary = V.allocSummary;
const allocTitle = V.allocTitle;
const allocErrText = (l: any) => V.allocErrText(l, lines);
const allocFactText = V.allocFactText;
const qtyFactText = (l: any) => V.qtyFactText(l, lines);
const rowFlag = (l: any) => V.rowFlag(l, lines, A);

const excludedLabels = V.excludedLabels;
const scopeText = V.scopeText;

/* ── Local to this surface — they read the page's own line list ── */
const labelOfId = (id: string) => lines.find((l) => l.id === id)?.label ?? id;

/* ── Grouping: a flat item list per category so the ROW markup is written once,
   whether or not the category is split into sub-blocks. ── */
const linesIn = (catKey: string, blockKey?: string) =>
  lines.filter((l) => l.category === catKey && (blockKey ? l.block === blockKey : true));
const itemsFor = (cat: any): any[] =>
  cat.blocks
    ? cat.blocks.flatMap((b: any) => [
        { kind: 'block', block: b },
        ...linesIn(cat.key, b.key).map((line: any) => ({ kind: 'line', line })),
      ])
    : linesIn(cat.key).map((line: any) => ({ kind: 'line', line }));


const t0 = priced.totals;
---

<section class="cbf-lct stack" data-gap="md" data-lct>
  {heading && (
    <header class="repel" data-gap="sm">
      <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
        <h2 class="typography-heading-md">{heading}</h2>
        {lede && <p class="cbf-lct__lede">{lede}</p>}
      </span>
      {/* ── Two ways in, and neither is the fallback ──────────────────────────
          Whether Excel import is the primary path with this form as backup, or
          the reverse, is not decided — and for change management the system has
          to support both. So import sits in the grid's own header, not behind a
          menu: a vendor who budgets in a workbook brings it here, and the lines
          land in this same grid, where every check runs on them. */}
      <span data-vendor-only>
        <EsaButton size="sm" variant="secondary" appearance="outline" icon="upload" data-open-import>
          Import from Excel
        </EsaButton>
      </span>
    </header>
  )}

  <!-- overflow stays visible at desktop on purpose: the allocation esa-popover,
       the esa-select panels and the add-line esa-dropdown-menu all position
       absolutely and cannot escape a clipping ancestor (same finding as
       cbf-lib-pi-documents). Narrow viewports fall back to horizontal scrolling
       and accept the clipping. -->
  <div class="cbf-lct__scroll">
    <!-- There is deliberately NO global <thead>. One header for nine categories
         forced every section to answer to the same three words, and "Quantity"
         means months here, nights there, and nothing at all in Fringe. Each
         category captions its own columns instead — see `columns` in
         src/data/lib-entry.mjs. The three columns that DON'T move (Amount, Work
         element, the row action) keep one name throughout, because the money
         column has to line up with the subtotals and the table foot. -->
    <table class="cbf-lct__table" aria-label="Line item budget cost lines">
      {categories.map((cat: any) => (
        <tbody data-cat={cat.key} data-split={cat.key === 'personnel-salary' ? (personnelSplit.on ? 'on' : 'off') : undefined}>
          {/* ── Category header: label, note, subtotal, and the inline add ── */}
          <tr class="cbf-lct__cathead">
            <th scope="colgroup" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
              <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                <span class="cbf-lct__cat-label">{cat.label}</span>
                {cat.note && <span class="cbf-lct__note">{cat.note}</span>}

                {/* ── Budget-level settings, where the lines they govern start ──
                    Personnel carries the DEFAULT fringe rate every position
                    follows unless its line overrides it, and the choice of
                    whether to split admin from technical at all. Supplies carries
                    the contract's capitalization threshold, which varies by
                    contract and so is the vendor's to state. Each sits on its own
                    section head rather than in a settings panel, because a rate
                    read beside the lines it prices is a rate that gets checked. */}
                {cat.key === 'personnel-salary' && (
                  <span class="cluster" data-gap="md" data-cat-controls>
                    <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--pct"
                        size="sm"
                        label="Default fringe"
                        suffix="%"
                        data-budget-field="fringeDefault"
                        value={V.fringeDefaultText()}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-fringe-default-note>{V.fringeDefaultNote(lines)}</span>
                    </span>
                    <span data-vendor-only>
                      <esa-switch-toggle
                        label="Split administrative and technical"
                        data-split-toggle
                        checked={personnelSplit.on || undefined}
                      ></esa-switch-toggle>
                    </span>
                    <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="fringe-default">
                      {`Default fringe ${V.fringeDefaultText()}%`}
                    </span>
                  </span>
                )}
                {cat.key === 'supplies' && (
                  <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-cat-controls>
                    <esa-text-field
                      class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--threshold"
                      size="sm"
                      label="Capitalization threshold"
                      prefix="$"
                      data-budget-field="threshold"
                      value={numText(contract.capitalizationThreshold)}
                    ></esa-text-field>
                    <span class="cbf-lct__note">Per item.</span>
                    <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="threshold">
                      {`Capitalization threshold ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} per item`}
                    </span>
                  </span>
                )}
              </span>
            </th>
            <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
              <strong class="cbf-lct__money" data-cat-total={cat.key}>{usd(priced.byCategory[cat.key] ?? 0)}</strong>
            </td>
            <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2">
              {cat.derived ? (
                /* A generated section has nothing to add TO. The affordance it
                   owes the vendor is not "Add line" but where the lines come
                   from — so the cell says that instead of offering a control
                   that would create a row the next recompute deletes. */
                <span class="cbf-lct__note">Edit fringe on the salary lines.</span>
              ) : cat.key === 'travel' ? (
                /* Two entry points, because travel is two things. A TRIP is a
                   container: it carries the destination and the occurrence
                   count that every cost under it would otherwise repeat, and it earns its keep the moment there is more than
                   one line. A single cab fare has none of that to say, and
                   making the vendor invent a journey to hold it is the cost
                   this second control removes. */
                <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                  <EsaButton size="sm" variant="primary" appearance="outline" icon="plus" data-add-trip>
                    Add trip
                  </EsaButton>
                  <esa-dropdown-menu position="below-end" data-add-travel={NO_TRIP}>
                    <EsaButton size="sm" variant="secondary" appearance="soft" icon="plus">
                      Add line without a trip
                    </EsaButton>
                  </esa-dropdown-menu>
                </span>
              ) : cat.shapes.length > 1 || cat.blocks || cat.kinds ? (
                /* More than one legal kind (or more than one sub-block): the add
                   affordance has to let the vendor pick which one, so it is a menu. */
                <esa-dropdown-menu position="below-end" data-add-menu={cat.key}>
                  <EsaButton size="sm" variant="primary" appearance="outline" icon="plus">Add line</EsaButton>
                </esa-dropdown-menu>
              ) : (
                <EsaButton
                  size="sm"
                  variant="primary"
                  appearance="outline"
                  icon="plus"
                  data-add-line={`${cat.key}||${cat.shapes[0]}`}
                >Add line</EsaButton>
              )}
            </td>
          </tr>

          {/* ── This category's OWN column captions ── */}
          <tr class="cbf-lct__colhead">
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-line">{cat.columns.line}</th>
            {cat.columns.span ? (
              <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-span" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS - 1}>{cat.columns.span}</th>
            ) : (
              <>
                <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-qty">
                  {cat.columns.qty ?? <span class="cbf-lct__sr">This section has no quantity column</span>}
                </th>
                {/* A section without a fringe cell spans its rate caption over
                    the gap rather than captioning a column it does not fill. */}
                <th
                  scope="col"
                  class="cbf-lct__col-rate"
                  colspan={cat.columns.fringe ? undefined : 2}
                >{cat.columns.rate}</th>
                {cat.columns.fringe && (
                  <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-fringe">{cat.columns.fringe}</th>
                )}
              </>
            )}
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-num">Amount</th>
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-we">Work element</th>
            <th scope="col" class="cbf-lct__col-act"><span class="cbf-lct__sr">Row actions</span></th>
          </tr>

          {itemsFor(cat).map((item: any) =>
            item.kind === 'block' ? (
              /* ── Sub-block header with its OWN subtotal ────────────────────
                 Personnel uses this for Technical/Administrative, and Supplies
                 & Equipment for Supplies/Capital Equipment — one section, split
                 on one axis, each side subtotaled. Travel uses the SAME
                 machinery for trips, and a trip head carries more: it owns the
                 facts every line under it would otherwise repeat — locality
                 and window — plus `occurrences`, the multiplier
                 that turns "a quarterly meeting" into one trip instead of four
                 unrelated rows. Raise it and every line below moves together. ── */
              <tr
                class="cbf-lct__blockhead"
                data-blockhead={item.block.key}
                data-trip={item.block.trip ? item.block.key : undefined}
              >
                <th scope="rowgroup" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
                  <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                    <span class="cbf-lct__block-label">{item.block.label}</span>

                    {/* A sub-block that states a RULE says it here — the two
                        equipment classes do, because which one an item lands in
                        is not a filing detail: it decides whether the indirect
                        rate reaches it and whether it becomes tracked property.
                        Office/Field never needed a sentence; this axis does. */}
                    {item.block.note && (
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note">{item.block.note}</span>
                    )}

                    {item.block.trip && (
                      <>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-trip-cell="window">
                          {item.block.trip.window}
                        </span>
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="sm" data-trip-controls>
                          {/* Destination is TYPED, not picked. The GSA schedule
                              prices a few hundred localities separately and covers
                              every other place with one standard CONUS row — so a
                              list of listed localities would tell a vendor they may
                              only travel where GSA itemises, which is the opposite
                              of how per diem works. A weir on Rock Creek is a real
                              destination that simply prices at CONUS. */}
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--dest"
                            size="sm"
                            label="Destination"
                            data-trip-field="locality"
                            value={item.block.trip.locality}
                            placeholder="City, ST"
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--trip"
                            size="sm"
                            label="Times it happens"
                            data-trip-field="occurrences"
                            value={String(item.block.trip.occurrences)}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                        </span>
                        {/* Why the trip is on the budget — the first question a
                            COR asks of travel, and one the destination alone
                            does not answer. One line, not a narrative. */}
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--purpose"
                          size="sm"
                          label="Purpose of travel"
                          data-trip-field="purpose"
                          value={item.block.trip.purpose ?? ''}
                          placeholder="What the trip is for"
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span data-trip-dest-err hidden={!!(item.block.trip.locality ?? '').trim()}>
                          <EsaFieldError message="Add a destination — every trip needs one." icon />
                        </span>
                        {/* COR lens: the trip as facts, not as an editor. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-trip-fact>
                          {countOf(item.block.trip.occurrences, 'trips')}
                        </span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-trip-purpose-fact>
                          {item.block.trip.purpose ? `Purpose: ${item.block.trip.purpose}` : 'No purpose given'}
                        </span>
                        {/* The whole journey's cost, when a meeting is linked to
                            it — what the trip is FOR, priced with what it costs
                            to get there. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-trip-cost hidden={!V.tripCostText(item.block.key, lines, A)}>
                          {V.tripCostText(item.block.key, lines, A)}
                        </span>
                      </>
                    )}
                  </span>
                </th>
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
                  <span class="cbf-lct__money" data-block-total={`${cat.key}:${item.block.key}`}>
                    {usd(priced.byBlock[`${cat.key}:${item.block.key}`] ?? 0)}
                  </span>
                </td>
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell">
                  {(item.block.trip || item.block.standalone) && (
                    /* Adding INSIDE a trip: the menu is the travel kinds, because
                       the kind is what the vendor is actually choosing. The
                       ungrouped block gets the same menu — it is a block like any
                       other, and that is the whole reason it IS one. */
                    <esa-dropdown-menu position="below-end" data-add-travel={item.block.key}>
                      <EsaButton size="sm" variant="secondary" appearance="soft" icon="plus">
                        Add travel line
                      </EsaButton>
                    </esa-dropdown-menu>
                  )}
                </td>
                {/* The head's delete keeps the ROW-ACTION column, so every trash
                    icon on this grid sits on one vertical line — a trip delete
                    that floated in beside the add button read as part of the add
                    affordance rather than as the head's own action.

                    A trip is a CONTAINER of costs, so it takes the lines filed in
                    it with it: there is no such thing as a travel line with no
                    trip to stand on. A trip carrying lines asks first; an empty
                    one just goes. */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--act">
                  {item.block.trip && (
                    <span data-delete-trip>
                      <EsaIconButton variant="chrome" iconOnly
                        icon="trash-2"
                        size="sm"
                        label={`Delete trip — ${item.block.label}`}
                      />
                    </span>
                  )}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ) : (
              <tr
                data-line={item.line.id}
                data-shape={item.line.shape}
                data-cat={cat.key}
                data-block={blockOf(item.line)}
                data-flag={rowFlag(item.line)}
              >
                {/* ── 1. The line's IDENTITY — and the mark, if it carries one ──
                     Four categories know what a line is called better than the
                     vendor does, so they replace free text with the thing that
                     actually decides the name: a position off the roster, a
                     travel kind, a vehicle kind, or — for fringe — the salary
                     line it follows. All five variants ship on every row and one
                     is revealed, which is what keeps a CLONED row correct when it
                     is added to, or moved into, a different category. */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--line">
                  {/* The grab handle leads the row because ORDER is a property of
                      the row, not of any one of its values — and the row's left
                      edge is where a vendor reaches for it. It is quiet until the
                      row is hovered or focused: a permanent column of grips down
                      a nine-category budget competes with the money.

                      Not on a derived row. A fringe line's position in its
                      section is a consequence of the salary line it follows —
                      syncFringeRows re-appends the whole section in list order on
                      every recompute — so a handle there would be an offer the
                      next keystroke revokes. */}
                  <div class="cbf-lct__grab">
                    {derivedRow(item.line) ? (
                      <span class="cbf-lct__grab-void" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    ) : (
                      <CbfDragHandle size="xs" label={displayLabel(item.line) || 'this line'} />
                    )}
                  <div class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
                    <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-ident="label" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'label'}>
                      {/* Two categories name a KIND beside the free-text name —
                          meetings & training (registration, subscription,
                          stipend) and other direct costs (honorarium). The kind
                          fixes the unit; the name says which course or whose
                          honorarium. */}
                      <span data-kind-pick="meetings" hidden={item.line.category !== 'meetings'}>
                        <esa-select
                          class="cbf-lct__select"
                          size="sm"
                          data-meeting-kind-select
                          placeholder="Pick a kind"
                          aria-label={`Kind of training cost — ${item.line.label || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-select>
                      </span>
                      <span data-kind-pick="other" hidden={item.line.category !== 'other'}>
                        <esa-select
                          class="cbf-lct__select"
                          size="sm"
                          data-other-kind-select
                          placeholder="Pick a kind"
                          aria-label={`Kind of cost — ${item.line.label || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-select>
                      </span>
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--label"
                        size="md"
                        data-field="label"
                        value={item.line.label}
                        placeholder="Describe this cost line"
                        aria-label={`Cost line description — ${item.line.label || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      {/* ── A meeting linked to the trip that gets there ──────
                          Registration lives here and the travel lives in Travel —
                          two categories apart, so nobody could see what the
                          conference costs in full. Linking the two keeps each
                          cost in the category the funder reads it in, and puts
                          the sum on the trip head. */}
                      <span data-trip-link hidden={item.line.category !== 'meetings'}>
                        <esa-select
                          class="cbf-lct__select"
                          size="sm"
                          data-trip-link-select
                          placeholder="Not linked to a trip"
                          aria-label={`Trip for this meeting — ${item.line.label || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-select>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="trip-link" hidden={!item.line.linkedTrip}>
                        {item.line.linkedTrip ? `Travel: ${tripLabel(tripOf(item.line.linkedTrip))} trip` : ''}
                      </span>
                    </span>

                    {/* ── The position: typed here, referenced everywhere after ──
                        Positions are defined BY this LIB, not chosen from a list
                        BPA supplies — a closed roster asserted we know the
                        sponsor's org chart, and a council whose staff didn't
                        match had nowhere to put them. So the vendor types the
                        title, and typing it is what creates the position.

                        It is a combobox rather than a plain text box because the
                        SECOND line for a position must be able to reuse the first
                        one's title exactly: retyping it by hand invites the
                        near-miss ("Seasonal Tech") that would silently split the
                        position in two and generate it a second fringe line. So
                        the titles already on this budget are offered, and a term
                        that matches none of them offers to become a new one. */}
                    <span data-ident="position" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'position'}>
                      <esa-combobox
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        mode="autocomplete"
                        data-position-combo
                        placeholder="Type a position title"
                        aria-label={`Position title — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-combobox>
                      <span data-position-err hidden={!!positionKey(displayLabel(item.line))}>
                        <EsaFieldError message="Name the position. Its fringe line and any linked lines refer back to it." icon />
                      </span>

                      {/* The type is NOT printed on the row: it belongs to the
                          position, and the sub-block heading this row sits under
                          already names it. Printing it again per row said the
                          same word down the whole block. */}
                    </span>

                    {/* The travel kind decides the units AND the schedule scope. */}
                    <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-ident="travel-kind" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'travel-kind'}>
                      <esa-select
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        data-travel-kind-select
                        placeholder={travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.label ?? 'Pick a travel kind'}
                        aria-label={`Travel kind — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-select>
                      {/* A vendor-defined kind says what it is — the kind list
                          could not, which is why it was chosen. */}
                      <span data-desc hidden={!V.takesDesc(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--label"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="desc"
                          value={item.line.desc ?? ''}
                          placeholder="What is this travel cost?"
                          aria-label={`Description — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                      </span>

                      {/* ── The one fact an ungrouped line cannot inherit ──────
                          A line inside a trip takes its destination from the trip
                          head, where it is typed once for every line under it. A
                          line on its own has nowhere else to carry it, so it
                          carries it here — same field, same CONUS resolution,
                          said for one line instead of a group. It is hidden the
                          moment the line joins a trip, because then the head is
                          the only honest place to change it. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs" data-travel-dest hidden={!isLooseTravel(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--dest"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="locality"
                          value={item.line.locality ?? ''}
                          placeholder="Destination — City, ST"
                          aria-label={`Destination — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--dest"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="purpose"
                          value={item.line.purpose ?? ''}
                          placeholder="Purpose of travel"
                          aria-label={`Purpose of travel — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span data-dest-err hidden={!!(item.line.locality ?? '').trim()}>
                          <EsaFieldError message="Add a destination — every travel cost needs one." icon />
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="purpose" hidden={!isLooseTravel(item.line) || !item.line.purpose}>
                        {item.line.purpose ? `Purpose: ${item.line.purpose}` : ''}
                      </span>
                    </span>

                    {/* The vehicle kind decides the shape: schedule-rated or typed. */}
                    <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-ident="vehicle-kind" hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'vehicle-kind'}>
                      <esa-select
                        class="cbf-lct__select"
                        size="md"
                        data-vehicle-kind-select
                        placeholder={vehicleKind(item.line.vehicleKind)?.label ?? 'Pick a vehicle kind'}
                        aria-label={`Vehicle kind — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                      ></esa-select>
                      {/* ── The vehicle TYPE, off the controlled list ─────────
                          The detail a COR needs to check the class against the
                          work, and the key the GSA rate is looked up by. Searched
                          rather than scrolled — twenty types is past what a
                          plain dropdown reads well. */}
                      <span data-vehicle-type hidden={!V.takesVehicleType(item.line)}>
                        <esa-combobox
                          class="cbf-lct__select"
                          size="md"
                          mode="autocomplete"
                          data-vehicle-type-select
                          placeholder="Pick the vehicle type"
                          search-placeholder="Search vehicle types…"
                          aria-label={`Vehicle type — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-combobox>
                        <span data-vehicle-type-err hidden={!vehicleKind(item.line.vehicleKind)?.needsType || !!item.line.vehicleType}>
                          <EsaFieldError message="Pick a vehicle type. It sets the GSA rate and lets the COR check the class." icon />
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      <span data-desc hidden={!V.takesDesc(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--label"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="desc"
                          value={item.line.desc ?? ''}
                          placeholder="What is this vehicle cost?"
                          aria-label={`Description — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                      </span>
                    </span>

                    {/* Fringe names itself after the position it is a percentage
                        of — there is nothing here for a vendor to decide. */}
                    <span
                      class="cbf-lct__derived"
                      data-ident="derived"
                      data-cell="ident-derived"
                      hidden={identOf(item.line) !== 'derived'}
                    >{displayLabel(item.line)}</span>

                    <span class="cbf-lct__fact cbf-lct__fact--label" data-fact="label">{displayLabel(item.line)}</span>

                    {/* ── The vendor's own accounting code ─────────────────────
                        Every line, so an invoice can be lined up against the
                        budget it bills. Small and last in the cell: it prices
                        nothing, and it is read when matching, not when building.
                        A generated fringe line has no field of its own — it
                        carries its salary lines' code. */}
                    <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-acct>
                      <span data-acct-edit hidden={derivedRow(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--code"
                          size="sm"
                          prefix="Code"
                          data-field="acctCode"
                          value={item.line.acctCode ?? ''}
                          placeholder="Accounting code"
                          aria-label={`Accounting code — ${displayLabel(item.line) || 'new line'}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-acct-derived hidden={!derivedRow(item.line)}>
                        {V.acctFactText(item.line, lines)}
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact cbf-lct__note" data-fact="acct" hidden={derivedRow(item.line)}>
                        {V.acctFactText(item.line, lines)}
                      </span>
                      {/* Quiet, not a warning: a vendor-defined line is allowed.
                          The badge is what keeps it from blending into the
                          standard kinds when the COR reads down the section. */}
                      <span data-vendor-badge hidden={!V.vendorDefined(item.line)}>
                        <EsaBadge value="Vendor-defined" variant="secondary" size="sm" />
                      </span>
                    </span>

                    {/* No second line under the name. The slot used to carry two
                        unrelated things — a kind's RULE on travel and vehicle
                        rows, and free text on every other row that nothing in
                        this UI could type — so it read as an editable detail on
                        some rows, an uneditable one on others, and was absent on
                        the rest. The kind's rule belongs to the kind picker,
                        which states it as help text. */}

                    {/* The COR's mark, on the line it disputes. Hidden from the
                        vendor until the review is issued — an unfinished review
                        read as a work list is worse than no review. */}
                    <span
                      class="cbf-lct__mark stack"
                      data-gap="3xs"
                      data-mark
                      data-sev={flagOf(item.line)?.severity ?? ''}
                      hidden={!flagOf(item.line) || !reviewIssued}
                    >
                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                        <span data-mark-sev={SEVERITIES.REQUIRED}>
                          <EsaBadge
                            value={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].short}
                            variant={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].variant}
                            size="sm"
                          />
                        </span>
                        <span data-mark-sev={SEVERITIES.QUESTION}>
                          <EsaBadge
                            value={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].short}
                            variant={severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].variant}
                            size="sm"
                          />
                        </span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__mark-reason" data-mark-reason>
                          {flagOf(item.line) ? reasonLabel(flagOf(item.line)!.reason) : ''}
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__mark-note" data-mark-note>
                        {flagOf(item.line)?.note ?? ''}
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-mark-by>
                        {flagOf(item.line) ? `${flagOf(item.line)!.author} · ${flagOf(item.line)!.date}` : ''}
                      </span>
                    </span>
                  </div>
                  </div>
                </td>

                {/* ── 2. Quantity — collapsed away entirely for shapes C and D ──
                     There is no "rate source" column: what governs a rate lives in
                     the Rate cell, beside the number it produces. */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.LUMP ? (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--void" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS - 1}>
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note">No quantity, no rate — one amount.</span>
                      {/* ── The one way left to defeat the sort ────────────────
                          Equipment files itself from its per-item cost, and a
                          lump has none — so a lump is where a capitalizable item
                          can still hide. Below the threshold it cannot be hiding
                          one and this says nothing; at or above it, the line has
                          to itemize before the sort can reach inside. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-cap-lump hidden={!capLumpOpen(item.line)}>
                        <span><EsaBadge value="Can’t test per item" variant="info" size="sm" /></span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cap-lump-err>{capLumpText(item.line)}</span>
                      </span>
                      {/* A lump has no per-item cost for the threshold to read,
                          so where it sits is ONLY ever the vendor's call — and
                          the same move an itemized line has is offered here. */}
                      <span data-cap-toggle data-vendor-only hidden={!onThreshold(item.line)}>
                        <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft">
                          {capToggleText(item.line)}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cap-lump-filed hidden={!onThreshold(item.line)}>
                        {onThreshold(item.line) ? capWhyText(item.line) : ''}
                      </span>
                      {item.line.sheet && (
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                          {/* The seam: a lump HERE, a full recursed category set THERE. */}
                          <EsaButton
                            size="sm"
                            variant="secondary"
                            appearance="soft"
                            icon="external-link"
                            data-open-sheet={item.line.sheet}
                            data-open-sheet-line={item.line.id}
                          >Open subcontract sheet</EsaButton>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note">Itemized on its own sheet — no indirect applied there.</span>
                        </span>
                      )}
                    </div>
                  </td>
                ) : item.line.shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE ? (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--void">
                    <span class="cbf-lct__void-mark">No quantity</span>
                  </td>
                ) : item.line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL ? (
                  /* ── Travel quantity: occurrences × count × duration ─────────
                     Occurrences is the only one RESOLVED rather than typed, and
                     it is never per-line: a trip that happens six times makes
                     every cost in it happen six times, and a cost that happens a
                     different number of times is a different trip.

                     The count and the duration are this line's own. An earlier
                     pass held the count on the trip as "travelers" and gave every
                     row an override button — but a lodging line counts ROOMS, a
                     mileage line counts VEHICLES, and per diem counts TRAVELERS.
                     Those are three different numbers, so one shared field could
                     not be right for all of them, and the button next to it was
                     the design admitting so. A new line seeds from the last line
                     in the trip counting the SAME unit instead. Both units are
                     fixed by the kind rather than spelled by the vendor. */
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--qty">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      {/* The multiplication is NOT restated for the vendor: the
                          two boxes below say "2 rooms × 1 night" already, and a
                          sentence repeating them back is a second thing to read
                          and a second thing to keep in step. It survives as the
                          COR's rendering, where the boxes are gone and this IS
                          the value — and where it is also the only place the
                          trip's occurrence multiplier appears on the row. */}
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-cell="travel-chain">
                        {travelChain(item.line)}
                      </span>

                      {/* ── ONE line, because it is ONE multiplication ────────
                          "2 rooms" and "1 night" are not two facts stacked up,
                          they are the two halves of 2 × 1, and stacking them put
                          a line break where the × belongs — so the eye read two
                          quantities and had to reassemble the product. The count
                          and the duration therefore share a single cluster, and
                          the qty column is sized to hold both whole (see
                          .cbf-lct__col-qty). It wraps rather than clipping if a
                          unit word is long enough to need it. */}
                      <span class="cluster cbf-lct__qty-chain" data-gap="2xs">
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-travel-count>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                            size="md"
                            data-field="count"
                            value={numText(travelCount(item.line))}
                            aria-label={`Count — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-cell="travel-count-unit">
                            {travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.countLabel ?? ''}
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        {/* Duration — always typed, unit always fixed by the kind. */}
                        <span
                          class="cluster"
                          data-gap="2xs"
                          data-travel-duration
                          hidden={!travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.durationLabel}
                        >
                          <span class="cbf-lct__times" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
                          <esa-text-field
                            class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                            size="md"
                            data-field="duration"
                            value={numText(item.line.duration)}
                            aria-label={`Duration — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          ></esa-text-field>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-cell="travel-duration-unit">
                            {travelKind(item.line.travelKind)?.durationLabel ?? ''}
                          </span>
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      {/* ── M&IE: the first and last travel day, at 75% ────────
                          Its own line under the chain, not a third term in it:
                          it is ADDED to the full days rather than multiplied in,
                          and "(3 full + 2 travel days)" reads as a sum only when
                          the plus sits at the start of a line. */}
                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-travel-partial hidden={!V.hasPartialDays(item.line)}>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__times" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                          size="md"
                          data-field="partialDays"
                          value={numText(item.line.partialDays ?? 0)}
                          aria-label={`Travel days at 75% — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__unit">{V.partialLabelOf(item.line) || 'travel days at 75%'}</span>
                      </span>
                      {/* No separate COR mirror here: the resolved chain above IS
                          the value, and it is shown to both roles. */}
                    </div>
                  </td>
                ) : (
                  <>
                    {/* ── 3. Quantity (typed, or DERIVED when it follows a line) ── */}
                    <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--qty">
                      <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                        {/* ── ONE line, because it is ONE multiplication ────────
                            "12" "months" "× 3" "vehicles" is a product, not four
                            facts stacked up, and a line break where the × belongs
                            makes the eye read two quantities and reassemble them.
                            Travel was fixed first because that is where it was
                            noticed; the same stacking was in every category that
                            can carry a second quantity, so the fix belongs to the
                            pattern rather than to the one cell it was spotted in.
                            It wraps rather than clipping when both units are open
                            text boxes and the column cannot hold all four. */}
                        <span class="cluster cbf-lct__qty-chain" data-gap="2xs">
                          <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-qty-typed hidden={!!item.line.qtyFrom}>
                            <esa-text-field
                              class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                              size="md"
                              data-field="qty"
                              value={numText(item.line.qty)}
                              aria-label={`Quantity — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                            ></esa-text-field>
                            {/* The unit is only a question where the category leaves
                                it open. A vehicle kind already fixed it to months,
                                so it reads as a value — not as an empty box the
                                vendor has to guess the house word for. */}
                            <esa-text-field
                              class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--unit"
                              size="md"
                              data-field="qtyUnit"
                              data-unit-open
                              value={item.line.qtyUnit ?? ''}
                              placeholder="Unit"
                              hidden={unitLocked(item.line)}
                              aria-label={`Unit — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                            ></esa-text-field>

                            {/* ── A personnel unit has to be TIME, so it is a list ──
                                This was a free-text box seeded with "months" sitting
                                on top of a contract-period gate and a column caption
                                that both assumed months no matter what was typed
                                into it — so a line could read "hrs" and still be
                                measured as though it read months. Off a closed list
                                the number and the gate finally mean the same thing,
                                and the unit can caption the rate beside it. */}
                            <span data-unit-select hidden={!unitPicked(item.line)}>
                              <esa-select
                                class="cbf-lct__select cbf-lct__select--unit"
                                size="md"
                                data-time-unit-select
                                placeholder={timeUnit(item.line).plural}
                                aria-label={`Time unit — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                              ></esa-select>
                            </span>

                            <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-unit-locked hidden={!unitStatic(item.line)}>
                              {lockedUnit(item.line)}
                            </span>
                          </span>

                          {/* ── How many people, at this time and rate ──────────
                              A NAMED second multiplicand, personnel only, default
                              1 — CORs asked for it so three seasonal technicians
                              are one line. The period check above still reads the
                              time per person. */}
                          <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-staff hidden={!isSalaryLine(item.line)}>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__times" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
                            <esa-text-field
                              class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty cbf-lct__field--staff"
                              size="md"
                              data-field="staff"
                              value={V.staffText(item.line)}
                              aria-label={`Staff on this line — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                            ></esa-text-field>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__unit">staff</span>
                          </span>

                          {/* The way BACK. A broken link that could not be remade
                              would just be a slower delete-and-re-add, and the
                              dependency is the thing worth keeping — so the line
                              remembers what it used to follow and offers it by
                              name. Only ever shown on a line that has detached. */}
                          <span
                            data-qty-refollow
                            hidden={!item.line.qtyFromWas || !!item.line.qtyFrom}
                          >
                            <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft" data-refollow-qty>
                              {item.line.qtyFromWas ? `Follow ${labelOfId(item.line.qtyFromWas)}` : 'Follow again'}
                            </EsaButton>
                          </span>

                          <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-qty2 hidden={item.line.qty2 == null}>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__times" aria-hidden="true">×</span>
                            <esa-text-field
                              class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--qty"
                              size="md"
                              data-field="qty2"
                              value={numText(item.line.qty2)}
                              aria-label={`Second quantity — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                            ></esa-text-field>
                            <esa-text-field
                              class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--unit"
                              size="md"
                              data-field="qty2Unit"
                              data-unit2-open
                              value={item.line.qty2Unit ?? ''}
                              placeholder="Unit"
                              hidden={unitLocked(item.line)}
                              aria-label={`Second unit — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                            ></esa-text-field>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__unit" data-unit2-locked hidden={!unitLocked(item.line)}>
                              {lockedUnit2(item.line)}
                            </span>
                          </span>

                          {/* There is deliberately NO "add a second quantity" here
                              any more. It was offered on Supplies, Meetings and
                              Rent, and all three are the same shape — a quantity
                              and a cost EACH — so a second multiplicand had no
                              meaning to carry: "6 units × 1 unit" is six units,
                              said twice. What it did carry was risk, since the
                              capitalization threshold divides by the first number
                              only, so a second one moved the money without moving
                              the per-item cost the sort reads.

                              A second quantity still exists, and is still priced:
                              a vehicle mileage line is months × miles/month. It
                              comes from the vehicle KIND, which knows both units,
                              rather than from a button that hands the vendor an
                              empty box and no word for what goes in it. */}
                        </span>

                        {/* The contract period is a ceiling on staffing months,
                            and it is arithmetic — so the form does it, in both
                            states, rather than leaving it to the reviewer. */}
                        <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-months-wrap hidden={!monthsTested(item.line)}>
                          <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                            <span data-months="pass" hidden={monthsOver(item.line)}>
                              <EsaBadge value="Within contract period" variant="success" />
                            </span>
                            <span data-months="fail" hidden={!monthsOver(item.line)}>
                              <EsaBadge value="Past contract period" variant="danger" />
                            </span>
                          </span>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-months-note>{monthsText(item.line)}</span>
                          <span data-months-err hidden={!monthsOver(item.line)}>
                            <EsaFieldError message={monthsErrText(item.line)} icon />
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        {/* Read-only, and it names the line it follows. */}
                        <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs" data-qty-derived hidden={!item.line.qtyFrom}>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-cell="derived-qty">
                            {qtyFmt(effectiveQty(item.line, lines))} {item.line.qtyUnit}
                          </span>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="derived-src">
                            {item.line.qtyFrom ? `Follows ${labelOfId(item.line.qtyFrom)}` : ''}
                          </span>
                          {/* The link has to be BREAKABLE. Following the position is
                              the right default — a phone budgeted for 12 months
                              against a position staffed for 10.5 is the drift this
                              prevents — but it is a default, not a fact: a device on
                              an annual plan outlives the season that uses it, and
                              without this the vendor's only way to say so was to
                              delete the line and re-add it in another category.
                              Breaking it keeps the number it resolved to, so the
                              cell never blanks. */}
                          <span data-qty-detach>
                            <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft" data-detach-qty>
                              Use my own quantity
                            </EsaButton>
                          </span>
                        </span>

                        {/* COR lens: the quantity as a value. A derived quantity
                            already reads as one, so it keeps its own cell. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="qty" hidden={!!item.line.qtyFrom}>
                          {qtyFactText(item.line)}
                        </span>
                      </div>
                    </td>
                  </>
                )}

                {/* ── 4. Rate — typed (A), referenced (B), a percentage (C) ── */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.QTY_RATE && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate" colspan={rateSpan(item.line)}>
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <esa-text-field
                        class:list={[
                          'cbf-lct__field',
                          'cbf-lct__field--rate',
                          /* A suffix is an addon INSIDE the box: it takes its own
                             width off the input rather than sitting beside it, so
                             a field sized for "$ 4,750" clips the moment "/month"
                             lands in it. The unit-bearing rate gets its own width. */
                          rateSuffixOf(item.line) && 'cbf-lct__field--rate-unit',
                        ]}
                        size="md"
                        prefix="$"
                        suffix={rateSuffixOf(item.line)}
                        data-field="rate"
                        value={numText(item.line.rate)}
                        aria-label={`${rateLabelOf(item.line)} — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      {/* The unit decides what the number beside it MEANS, so it
                          rides the box as a suffix — an hourly line reads
                          "$16.60 /hour" in place, not a caption underneath. */}
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="rate">{money(item.line.rate ?? 0)}</span>

                      {/* ── A typed vehicle rate, read against GSA ────────────
                          A commercial lease or a motor-pool vehicle is priced at
                          the lessor's or the pool's rate — but the GSA rate for
                          the same type is the yardstick a COR reads it against,
                          so it is stated, and a rate above it is noted. Noted,
                          not blocked: a vendor may pay more than GSA charges. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-compare hidden={!V.compareText(item.line)}>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-compare-text>{V.compareText(item.line)}</span>
                        <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-compare-above hidden={!V.compareAbove(item.line)}>
                          <span><EsaBadge value="Above the GSA rate" variant="info" size="sm" /></span>
                          <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-compare-note>{V.gsaErrText(item.line)}</span>
                        </span>
                      </span>

                      {/* ── A second rate for the same position ───────────────
                          A fiscal-year crossover, a COLA, a step increase: the
                          COR consensus was a second line with the same title.
                          This makes it one gesture — the time is split across
                          the two lines and the new one opens on its rate. */}
                      <span data-rate-step data-vendor-only hidden={!isSalaryLine(item.line)}>
                        <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft" icon="plus">Add another rate</EsaButton>
                      </span>

                      {/* ── Where this item filed itself, and what that costs ──
                          The per-item cost sits next to the rate that produces
                          it, and states the SECTION it is in plus the consequence
                          of that section — the indirect base and Property
                          Inventory. Underneath, the MOVE to the other section,
                          in both directions: thresholds and their exceptions
                          differ by contract, so placing an item is the vendor's
                          call, and a placement against the threshold is noted
                          for the COR rather than refused. */}
                      <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs" data-cap-wrap hidden={!thresholdTested(item.line)}>
                        <span data-cap="filed" hidden={capOverride(item.line)}>
                          <span class="stack" data-gap="3xs">
                            <span class="cbf-lct__filed" data-cap-filed>{capFiledText(item.line)}</span>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cap-why>{capWhyText(item.line)}</span>
                          </span>
                        </span>
                        <span data-cap="override" hidden={!capOverride(item.line)}>
                          <span class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                            <span data-cap-against hidden={!V.capAgainst(item.line, A)}>
                              <EsaBadge value="Moved by hand" variant="info" size="sm" />
                            </span>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cap-err>{capOverrideText(item.line)}</span>
                            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cap-why-ovr>{capWhyText(item.line)}</span>
                          </span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-vendor-only>
                          <span data-cap-toggle hidden={!capCanOverride(item.line)}>
                            <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft">
                              {capToggleText(item.line)}
                            </EsaButton>
                          </span>
                          <span data-cap-reset hidden={!V.capResetText(item.line)}>
                            <EsaButton size="xs" variant="ghost">{V.capResetText(item.line) || 'File by threshold'}</EsaButton>
                          </span>
                        </span>
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {/* ── 4b. Fringe — a column, because fringe rates are SCANNED ──
                     Fringe is not a budgeting choice; it is the organization's
                     rate applied to a position, and the vendor's only real input
                     is the percentage. It used to be a section of its own, which
                     cost three gestures where there were no decisions (create a
                     line, point it at a base, type a work-element split) and gave
                     all three a way to drift from the salary they follow.

                     It is a COLUMN rather than another line in the rate cell
                     because the question a reviewer actually asks of it — "why is
                     this one 22.3% when the rest are 35.1%" — is a question you
                     answer by reading DOWN, and a value stacked inside another
                     cell cannot be read down. Every other section spans its rate
                     cell across this gap, so none of them pays for the column.

                     The dollars sit BESIDE the rate, not under it: the row is
                     read left to right — the rate typed here, the dollars it
                     generates, then the line's own amount in the very next
                     column. Stacked under the field they read as a footnote
                     about the row rather than as the number they are. */}
                {hasFringeCell(item.line) && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--fringe">
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <div class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-fringe>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--pct"
                          size="md"
                          suffix="%"
                          data-field="fringePct"
                          value={fringePctText(item.line)}
                          aria-label={`Fringe rate — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="fringe">{fringeFactText(item.line)}</span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__derived cbf-lct__fringe-amt" data-fringe-note>{fringeAmtText(item.line)}</span>
                      </div>
                      {/* Following the default is the quiet state; an override
                          says so, because "why is this one different" is what a
                          reviewer reads this column for — and it is one click
                          back to the default. */}
                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs" data-fringe-override hidden={!V.fringeOverridden(item.line)}>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note">Own rate</span>
                        <span data-fringe-reset data-vendor-only>
                          <EsaButton size="xs" variant="ghost">{V.fringeResetText()}</EsaButton>
                        </span>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-fringe-follows hidden={V.fringeOverridden(item.line)}>Uses the default</span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {/* ── Travel's rate cell: typed, with a claim available ───────
                     Every travel rate is the vendor's own number. Three of the
                     six kinds have a published standard they may CLAIM instead,
                     and claiming it is an explicit act with two answers of its
                     own — which location, which fiscal year — because those are
                     what make the claim checkable a year later when the schedule
                     has moved. The claim supersedes the typed rate without
                     erasing it, so dropping it gives the vendor their number
                     back rather than a blank.

                     This replaces a cell where lodging and per diem were
                     schedule-rated by DEFAULT and the typed number was the
                     "override". That model priced from a destination the vendor
                     had typed for naming purposes, so a misspelt city quietly
                     cost the difference between a locality rate and the CONUS
                     floor, and no number on the row was one anybody had
                     entered. ── */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate" colspan={rateSpan(item.line)}>
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      {/* The rate, always present and always the vendor's —
                          while no claim supersedes it. A claim replaces the box
                          rather than disabling it: a greyed-out field the vendor
                          cannot use is a control asking to be tried, and the
                          number it held is stated in the dialog that made the
                          claim, where "what happens to my rate" is asked. */}
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--rate"
                        size="md"
                        prefix="$"
                        data-field="rate"
                        data-typed-rate
                        value={numText(item.line.rate)}
                        hidden={claimsStandard(item.line) || undefined}
                        aria-label={`Rate — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>

                      {/* Claimed: the rate, and that it is the standard one and
                          for which year. Two facts, because those are the two a
                          column of rates is READ for — is this number theirs or
                          the schedule's, and which schedule. The locality rides
                          the title and is re-answered in the dialog; it was a
                          third line of text on every claimed row and answered a
                          question nobody scanning the column was asking. */}
                      <span
                        class="cluster cbf-lct__claim"
                        data-gap="2xs"
                        data-standard-claim
                        hidden={!claimsStandard(item.line)}
                        title={standardSrcText(item.line)}
                      >
                        <span class="cbf-lct__ref" data-cell="standard-rate">
                          {standardAmountText(item.line)}
                        </span>
                        <span data-standard-badge>
                          <EsaBadge value={standardBadgeText(item.line)} variant="info" size="sm" />
                        </span>
                        {/* Seasonal lodging says which season it claimed — the
                            detail CORs check, and the one a flat badge hid. */}
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-standard-season hidden={!V.seasonText(item.line)}>
                          {V.seasonText(item.line)}
                        </span>
                        {/* The way back INTO the claim — its year and its
                            locality are changed where they were answered. */}
                        <span data-standard-edit>
                          <EsaIconButton variant="chrome" iconOnly
                            icon="pencil"
                            size="sm"
                            label={`Change the standard rate claim — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                          />
                        </span>
                      </span>

                      {/* Offered only where something is actually published. On
                          airfare, ground transport and registration the cell says
                          so instead, because "no standard available" and "you
                          chose not to claim one" are different facts. */}
                      <span data-standard-toggle hidden={!canClaimStandard(item.line)}>
                        <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft">
                          {claimsStandard(item.line) ? 'Use my own rate' : 'Use the standard rate'}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="typed-src" hidden={claimsStandard(item.line)}>
                        {typedSrcText(item.line)}
                      </span>

                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="travel-rate">{money(effectiveRate(item.line))}</span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__fact" data-fact="travel-rate-src">
                        {claimsStandard(item.line) ? standardSrcText(item.line) : typedSrcText(item.line)}
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate" colspan={rateSpan(item.line)}>
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      {/* There is no schedule-row picker any more. The vehicle
                          TYPE, picked off the controlled list in the identity
                          cell, IS the lookup — a vendor used to search the fleet
                          schedule for a row and could pick a class that did not
                          match the vehicle they named. */}
                      <span class="cbf-lct__ref" data-cell="ref-rate">{money(effectiveRate(item.line))}</span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="ref-src">{gsaSrcText(item.line)}</span>

                      <span class="cluster" data-gap="2xs">
                        <span data-gsa="pass" hidden={overridden(item.line) || !gsaOf(item.line)}>
                          <EsaBadge value="GSA rate" variant="success" size="sm" />
                        </span>
                        <span data-gsa="under" hidden={!overridden(item.line) || gsaOver(item.line)}>
                          <EsaBadge value="Your own rate" variant="info" size="sm" />
                        </span>
                        <span data-gsa="over" hidden={!gsaOver(item.line)}>
                          <EsaBadge value="Above the GSA rate" variant="info" size="sm" />
                        </span>
                      </span>

                      {/* ── The override: for vendors not paying the GSA rate ──
                          CORs asked for this flexibility. A rate above GSA is
                          stated plainly and noted for the COR; it no longer
                          fails the line. */}
                      <span data-override-wrap hidden={!overridden(item.line)}>
                        <esa-text-field
                          class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--rate"
                          size="md"
                          prefix="$"
                          data-field="rateOverride"
                          value={numText(item.line.rateOverride)}
                          aria-label={`Your rate — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        ></esa-text-field>
                      </span>
                      <span>
                        <EsaButton size="xs" variant="secondary" appearance="soft" data-toggle-override>
                          {overridden(item.line) ? 'Use the GSA rate' : 'Use my own rate'}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-gsa-err hidden={!gsaOver(item.line)}>{gsaErrText(item.line)}</span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {/* ── A derived row's rate cell: where the number CAME FROM ────
                     Nothing here is an input. The percentage was typed on a
                     salary row, the base is the sum of that position's salary
                     lines, and the only thing this cell owes the reader is the
                     route back — which is why it names the line to edit rather
                     than offering a control that would edit the wrong thing. */}
                {item.line.shape === SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE && (
                  <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--rate" colspan={rateSpan(item.line)}>
                    <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                      <span class="cbf-lct__ref" data-cell="fringe-pct">{fringeRateText(item.line)}</span>
                      <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-cell="fringe-src">{fringeSrcText(item.line)}</span>
                      {/* The dependency, made visible on the row itself. */}
                      <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-cell="base">
                        {money(baseAmountFor(item.line, lines, priced.amounts))} base ·
                        {' '}{baseLabelFor(item.line, lines)}
                      </span>
                    </div>
                  </td>
                )}

                {/* ── 5. Amount — typed only for a lump; derived everywhere else ── */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
                  {item.line.shape === SHAPES.LUMP ? (
                    <>
                      <esa-text-field
                        class="cbf-lct__field cbf-lct__field--amount"
                        size="md"
                        prefix="$"
                        data-field="amount"
                        value={numText(item.line.amount)}
                        aria-label={`Amount — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                      ></esa-text-field>
                      <strong class="cbf-lct__fact cbf-lct__money" data-fact="amount">
                        {usd(amt(item.line.id))}
                      </strong>
                    </>
                  ) : (
                    <strong class="cbf-lct__money" data-cell="amount">{usd(amt(item.line.id))}</strong>
                  )}
                </td>

                {/* ── 6. Work-element allocation — on EVERY row, whatever the shape ── */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--we">
                  <div class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
                    {/* A derived row's split is not its own — it is the split of
                        the salary lines it follows, inherited weighted by their
                        dollars. Offering an editor here would offer a way to make
                        the two disagree, which is the bug this whole section
                        stopped being able to have. So it reads as a value for
                        both roles, and the error below points at the row that can
                        actually fix it. */}
                    <span class="cbf-lct__derived" data-alloc-derived hidden={!derivedRow(item.line)}>
                      {allocFactText(item.line)}
                    </span>

                    {/* ── The split is ENTERED on a side sheet ──────────────
                        It used to be typed in a popover hanging off this button.
                        That is sized by its anchor and clipped by the viewport,
                        so it worked for this contract's six work elements and
                        would not have worked for twenty — and it had nowhere to
                        put "split evenly across all", which has to sit beside the
                        list it rewrites. What stays here is the summary and the
                        state, which is what the column is for: the row says how
                        the line is split, and opening the sheet is how it
                        changes. See cbf-lib-alloc-sheet. */}
                    <span data-alloc-editor hidden={derivedRow(item.line)}>
                      <span data-alloc-state="set" hidden={allocSum(item.line) !== 100}>
                        <EsaButton size="md" variant="secondary" appearance="outline" title={allocTitle(item.line)}>
                          {allocSummary(item.line)}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                      <span data-alloc-state="unset" hidden={allocSum(item.line) === 100}>
                        <EsaButton size="md" variant="warning" appearance="outline" title={allocTitle(item.line)}>
                          {allocSummary(item.line)}
                        </EsaButton>
                      </span>
                    </span>

                    {/* COR lens: the split as a value, not an editor. A derived
                        row already reads as one for both roles, so it keeps the
                        mirror out of the way rather than printing it twice. */}
                    <span
                      class="cbf-lct__fact"
                      data-fact="alloc"
                      title={allocTitle(item.line)}
                      hidden={derivedRow(item.line)}
                    >
                      {allocFactText(item.line)}
                    </span>

                    <span data-alloc-row-err hidden={allocSum(item.line) === 100}>
                      <EsaFieldError message={allocErrText(item.line)} icon />
                    </span>
                  </div>
                </td>

                {/* ── 7. Row action — delete for the vendor, MARK for the COR ──
                    The same slot, because they are the same thing from each side:
                    the one operation that role performs on a line it is looking
                    at. The mark editor opens on the row so the note is written
                    with the numbers it is about still on screen. ── */}
                <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--act">
                  {/* No delete on a derived row: it exists because a salary line
                      exists, and it stops existing when that one does. A button
                      here would be an offer the model cannot honour. */}
                  <span data-delete-line hidden={derivedRow(item.line)}>
                    <EsaIconButton variant="chrome" iconOnly icon="trash-2" size="sm" label={`Delete line — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`} />
                  </span>

                  <span data-mark-cell>
                    <esa-popover position="left" trigger="click" offset="6" data-mark-popover>
                      <span data-mark-state="off" hidden={!!flagOf(item.line)}>
                        <EsaIconButton variant="chrome" iconOnly
                          icon="pencil"
                          size="sm"
                          label={`Mark this line for the vendor — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        />
                      </span>
                      <span data-mark-state="on" hidden={!flagOf(item.line)}>
                        <EsaIconButton variant="chrome" iconOnly
                          icon="triangle-alert"
                          size="sm"
                          label={`Edit the mark on this line — ${displayLabel(item.line)}`}
                        />
                      </span>

                      <div slot="content" class="cbf-lct__markform stack" data-gap="sm" data-mark-panel>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__alloc-head">Mark this line for the vendor</span>
                        <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-mark-form-line>{displayLabel(item.line)}</span>
                        <esa-radio-group data-mark-sev-input size="md" label="Type of mark"></esa-radio-group>
                        <esa-select
                          data-mark-reason-input
                          size="md"
                          label="Reason"
                          placeholder="Pick a reason"
                        ></esa-select>
                        <esa-textarea
                          data-mark-note-input
                          label="Note to the vendor"
                          rows="3"
                          auto-resize
                          help-text="Say what to change, not just what’s wrong."
                          value={flagOf(item.line)?.note ?? ''}
                        ></esa-textarea>
                        <span class="repel" data-gap="sm">
                          <span data-mark-remove hidden={!flagOf(item.line)}>
                            <EsaButton size="sm" variant="ghost">Remove mark</EsaButton>
                          </span>
                          <EsaButton size="sm" variant="primary" data-mark-save>Save mark</EsaButton>
                        </span>
                      </div>
                    </esa-popover>
                  </span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ),
          )}
        </tbody>
      ))}

      {/* ── The close: direct, indirect base, indirect, and the ceiling test ── */}
      <tfoot class="cbf-lct__totals">
        {/* ── The fringe subtotal, for the lens that cannot see the section ────
             With Personnel — Fringe hidden from the vendor, adding up the
             section subtotals they CAN see comes out short by exactly this — and
             a ledger a vendor cannot add up is a ledger they will not trust.
             This is the same number the hidden section subtotals to; it just
             reports where the money went instead of listing the rows. The COR,
             who reads the section itself, does not need it repeated. */}
        <tr data-fringe-rollup>
          <th scope="row" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
            Personnel — fringe
            <span class="cbf-lct__note">
              Generated from the salary lines above, at the fringe rate on each.
            </span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num">
            <span data-cat-total="personnel-fringe">{usd(priced.byCategory['personnel-fringe'] ?? 0)}</span>
          </td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
            Direct cost
            <span class="cbf-lct__note">Sum of every cost line above.</span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><span data-total="direct">{usd(t0.direct)}</span></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr data-indirect-row>
          <th scope="row" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
            Indirect base
            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-indirect-scope>{scopeText(indirectConfig)}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><span data-total="indirectBase">{usd(t0.indirectBase)}</span></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr data-indirect-row>
          <th scope="row" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
            Indirect at <span data-indirect-rate>{pct(indirectConfig.rate)}</span>
            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-indirect-ref>{indirectConfig.referenceDoc}</span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><span data-total="indirect">{usd(t0.indirect)}</span></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="cbf-lct__grand" data-indirect-row>
          <th scope="row" colspan={ENTRY_COLUMNS}>
            Total this contract period
            <span class="cbf-lct__note" data-total-note>
              {pct(t0.pctOfValue, 1)} of the {usd(contract.contractValue)} Contract Value ·
              {' '}{usd(t0.remaining)} remaining
            </span>
          </th>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell cbf-lct__cell--num"><strong data-total="total">{usd(t0.total)}</strong></td>
          <td class="cbf-lct__cell" colspan="2"></td>
        </tr>
      </tfoot>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

{/* ── Deleting a trip that carries lines ─────────────────────────────────────
    A line delete is one row the vendor is looking at, so it just happens. A
    trip delete reaches costs that are not in front of them, and the count is the
    fact that decides whether they meant it — so the trip states what goes with
    it before it goes. An EMPTY trip never opens this: there is nothing to warn
    about.

    There are TWO ways out, because deleting a trip is now two different
    intentions. "The journey was wrong, the costs are real" keeps the lines and
    files them as ungrouped travel, each carrying away the destination it was
    pricing at. "None of this is happening" takes them with it. Before travel
    could be entered outside a trip there was only the second, and a vendor who
    meant the first had to retype every line. */}
<esa-dialog data-del-trip-dialog size="sm" heading="Delete this trip?">
  <p data-del-trip-body></p>
  <div slot="footer" class="cluster" data-gap="sm">
    <span data-del-trip-cancel>
      <EsaButton size="sm" variant="secondary" appearance="outline">Keep the trip</EsaButton>
    </span>
    <span data-del-trip-keep>
      <EsaButton size="sm" variant="secondary" appearance="soft">Delete trip, keep the lines</EsaButton>
    </span>
    <span data-del-trip-confirm>
      <EsaButton size="sm" variant="danger" icon="trash-2">Delete trip and its lines</EsaButton>
    </span>
  </div>
</esa-dialog>

<script>
  import * as V from '../../lib/lib-entry-view';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-text-field';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-select';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-combobox';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-popover';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-dropdown-menu';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-dialog';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-radio-group';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-textarea';
  import {
    SEVERITIES,
    REASONS,
    severityMeta,
    reasonLabel,
    reviewMeta,
    loadReview,
    saveReview,
    flagFor,
    isIssued,
    fireReview,
  } from '../../data/lib-review.mjs';
  import {
    SHAPES,
    categories,
    costLines,
    priceLines,
    effectiveRate,
    effectiveQty,
    baseAmountFor,
    baseLabelFor,
    dependentsOf,
    gsaRates,
    gsaRatesFor,
    gsaRatesForVehicleKind,
    gsaRate,
    isScheduleRated,
    weLabel,
    unallocatedLines,
    gsaFailingLines,
    thresholdMisfiled,
    untestableEquipmentLumps,
    monthsOverPeriod,
    perItemCost,
    onThreshold,
    thresholdTested,
    thresholdTargetFor,
    isClassOverride,
    canOverrideThreshold,
    autoFileEquipment,
    filingConsequence,
    blockOf,
    categoryOf,
    categoryFixedUnit,
    contract,
    contractMonths,
    positionKey,
    rosterFor,
    titleOfLine,
    PERSONNEL_TYPES,
    TIME_UNITS,
    timeUnit,
    isSalaryLine,
    timeInMonths,
    periodProblem,
    trips,
    tripOf,
    tripLabel,
    createTrip,
    removeTrip,
    emptiedTrips,
    NO_TRIP,
    isLooseTravel,
    TRAVEL_KINDS,
    travelKind,
    travelChain,
    travelCount,
    seedCountFor,
    travelLabel,
    travelLocality,
    hasStandardRate,
    standardNeedsLocality,
    defaultRateYear,
    fringeLabel,
    fringePctOf,
    fringeOnLine,
    isDerivedFringe,
    syncFringeLines,
    DEFAULT_FRINGE_PCT,
    hasFringeCell,
    rateSpan,
    VEHICLE_KINDS,
    vehicleKind,
    countOf,
    indirectConfig,
    usd,
    pct,
    qtyFmt,
    equipmentClassLabel,
    fringeDefaults,
    personnelSplit,
    gsaKeyForVehicle,
    meetingKind,
    otherKind,
    MEETING_KINDS,
    OTHER_KINDS,
    submitChecksFor,
    advisoriesFor,
    isVendorDefined,
    isFringeOverride,
    acctCodeOf,
} from '../../data/lib-entry.mjs';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-switch-toggle';
  import { initRowReorder, moveInList } from '../../lib/row-reorder';
  import { isBlank, blankConfig } from '../../data/lib-demo.mjs';

  const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-lct]');
  if (root) init(root);

  function init(root: HTMLElement) {
    /* Working copies — edits, added and deleted lines mutate THESE, never the
       module's exports, so every derived number stays honest across a repaint. */
    const lines: any[] = costLines.map((l: any) => ({ ...l, allocations: { ...(l.allocations ?? {}) } }));
    let cfg: any = { ...indirectConfig, appliesTo: [...indirectConfig.appliesTo] };
    let seq = 0;

    const $ = <T extends HTMLElement = HTMLElement>(sel: string, scope: ParentNode = root) =>
      scope.querySelector<T>(sel);
    const $$ = (sel: string, scope: ParentNode = root) =>
      Array.from(scope.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(sel));
    const rowOf = (id: string) => $(`tr[data-line="${id}"]`);
    const lineById = (id: string) => lines.find((l) => l.id === id);
    const num = (s: string) => {
      const v = parseFloat(String(s ?? '').replace(/[^0-9.\-]/g, ''));
      return Number.isFinite(v) ? v : 0;
    };

    /* ── View derivations — the SAME bodies the frontmatter uses ─────────────
       These used to be re-declared here, which is how this scope's qtyFactText
       lost the travel branch the SSR copy kept: the row server-rendered its
       "2 trips × 2 rooms × 1 night" chain and then degraded to a bare quantity on
       the first repaint. Now both scopes bind one body from lib-entry-view and
       differ only in what they pass it — this one passes the LIVE amounts and
       config, the frontmatter passes its static ones. */
    const A = () => priceLines(lines, cfg).amounts;

    const cents = V.cents;
    const money = V.money;
    const numText = V.numText;

    const gsaOf = V.gsaOf;
    const overridden = V.overridden;
    const gsaOver = V.gsaOver;
    const gsaSrcText = V.gsaSrcText;
    const gsaErrText = V.gsaErrText;
    const gsaLabel = V.gsaLabel;
    const claimsStandard = V.claimsStandard;
    const canClaimStandard = V.canClaimStandard;
    const standardSrcText = V.standardSrcText;
    const typedSrcText = V.typedSrcText;
    const standardAmountText = V.standardAmountText;
    const standardBadgeText = V.standardBadgeText;

    /* The cap family is called from paintRow, which already has the priced
       amounts in hand — so they stay explicit rather than re-pricing per call. */
    const perItem = (l: any, a: any) => V.perItem(l, a);
    const capOverride = V.capOverride;
    const capLumpOpen = (l: any, a: any) => V.capLumpOpen(l, a);
    const capPass = (l: any, a: any) => V.capPass(l, a);
    const capFiledText = (l: any, a: any) => V.capFiledText(l, a);
    const capWhyText = (l: any) => V.capWhyText(l, cfg);
    const capOverrideText = (l: any, a: any) => V.capOverrideText(l, a);
    const capLumpText = (l: any, a: any) => V.capLumpText(l, a);
    const capCanOverride = (l: any, a: any) => V.capCanOverride(l, a);
    const capToggleText = V.capToggleText;

    const monthsTested = V.monthsTested;
    const monthsOver = (l: any) => V.monthsOver(l, lines);
    const monthsText = (l: any) => V.monthsText(l, lines);
    const monthsErrText = (l: any) => V.monthsErrText(l, lines);
    const rateLabelOf = V.rateLabelOf;
    const rateSuffixOf = V.rateSuffixOf;


    const fringePctText = V.fringePctText;
    const fringeAmtText = (l: any, a: any) => V.fringeAmtText(l, lines, a);
    const fringeFactText = V.fringeFactText;
    const derivedRow = V.derivedRow;
    const fringeSrcText = (l: any) => V.fringeSrcText(l, lines);
    const fringeRateText = (l: any) => V.fringeRateText(l, lines);
    const derivedAllocErrText = (l: any) => V.derivedAllocErrText(l, lines);

    const identOf = V.identOf;
    const displayLabel = (l: any) => V.displayLabel(l, lines);
    
    const unitLocked = V.unitLocked;
    const lockedUnit = V.lockedUnit;
    const lockedUnit2 = V.lockedUnit2;
    const unitPicked = V.unitPicked;
    const unitStatic = V.unitStatic;

    const allocEntries = V.allocEntries;
    const allocSum = V.allocSum;
    const allocSummary = V.allocSummary;
    const allocTitle = V.allocTitle;
    const allocErrText = (l: any) => V.allocErrText(l, lines);
    const allocFactText = V.allocFactText;
    const qtyFactText = (l: any) => V.qtyFactText(l, lines);

    const excludedLabels = V.excludedLabels;
    const scopeText = V.scopeText;
    const acctFactText = (l: any) => V.acctFactText(l, lines);

    /* ── Local to this scope ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
       syncLabel MUTATES the line, so it is not a view derivation and does not
       belong in lib-entry-view — the frontmatter never calls it, because SSR
       renders labels that are already in step. */
    const labelOfId = (id: string) => lines.find((l) => l.id === id)?.label ?? id;
    /* Keep the stored label in step with a derived one, so the review panel —
       which addresses lines BY NAME — never quotes a name the grid stopped
       showing. A personnel line is excluded: its label IS the typed position
       title, so writing displayLabel back over it is a no-op at best. */
    const syncLabel = (l: any) => {
      if (l.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL || l.category === 'personnel-fringe' || l.category === 'vehicles') {
        l.label = displayLabel(l);
      }
    };


    /* ── Tiny DOM setters (esa-* internals are stable markup) ── */
    const show = (el: Element | null, on: boolean) => { if (el) (el as HTMLElement).hidden = !on; };
    const setText = (el: Element | null, text: string) => { if (el) el.textContent = text; };
    /** An esa-* affix is a reactive property, so it is set, not written as text. */
    const setSuffix = (el: Element | null, text: string) => { if (el) (el as any).suffix = text; };
    /** esa-field-error renders <p.esa-field-error><span.esa-field-error__text>. */
    const setErrText = (wrap: Element | null, text: string) =>
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-field-error__text') ?? null, text);
    /** esa-badge renders <span.esa-badge><span.esa-badge__text>. */
    const setBadgeText = (wrap: Element | null, text: string) =>
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-badge__text') ?? null, text);
    /** esa-button renders <span.esa-button><button><span.esa-button__label>. */
    const setBtnLabel = (wrap: Element | null, text: string, title?: string) => {
      setText(wrap?.querySelector('.esa-button__label') ?? null, text);
      if (title != null) wrap?.querySelector('button')?.setAttribute('title', title);
    };

    /* ── Select option sets ── */
    /* Scoped per LINE, and now per KIND — which is the tighter, truer filter.
       Scoping by category already stopped a travel line reaching a fleet lease
       row, but it still let a lodging line pick an M&IE rate, which is the error
       that actually happens. The kind narrows the schedule to the rows that kind
       can legally reference, so most lines see a handful of localities instead of
       the whole schedule. */
    const gsaOptionsFor = (line: any) => {
      const rows =
        line.category === 'vehicles' && line.vehicleKind
          ? gsaRatesForVehicleKind(line.vehicleKind)
          : gsaRatesFor(line.category);
      return rows.map((g: any) => ({
        label: `${g.location} · ${g.kind} · ${g.season}`,
        value: g.key,
      }));
    };
    /* Just the title. Appending "· Technical" overflowed the select and truncated
       the part that identifies the position — and the type already renders as its
       own line directly beneath, so the suffix said it twice and cost the title. */
    /* The position titles this budget has already defined. */
    const positionOptions = () => rosterFor(lines).map((p: any) => ({ label: p.title, value: p.key }));
    /* esa-combobox only commits values present in `options`, so "create" is an
       OPTION rather than free text escaping the control: on each debounced search
       a term matching no existing title is offered as one. Its label contains the
       term, so it survives the combobox's own label filter. */
    const NEW_POS = '__new-position__:';
    const positionOptionsFor = (term: string) => {
      const opts = positionOptions();
      const t = (term ?? '').trim();
      if (!t) return opts;
      const exists = opts.some((o: any) => o.label.toLowerCase() === t.toLowerCase());
      return exists ? opts : [{ label: `Use \u201c${t}\u201d`, value: NEW_POS + t }, ...opts];
    };
    /** Re-seed every personnel combobox \u2014 the roster is shared across the rows. */
    const refreshPositionOptions = () => {
      const opts = positionOptions();
      lines.filter(isSalaryLine).forEach((l) => {
        const tr = rowOf(l.id);
        const combo: any = tr && $('[data-position-combo]', tr);
        if (combo) { combo.options = opts; combo.value = l.staffKey ?? ''; }
      });
    };
    const timeUnitOptions = () => TIME_UNITS.map((u: any) => ({ label: u.plural, value: u.key }));
    const travelKindOptions = () => TRAVEL_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({ label: k.label, value: k.key }));
    const vehicleKindOptions = () => VEHICLE_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({ label: k.label, value: k.key }));
    /* The trip-link options are the trips THIS budget has, so they are rebuilt
       whenever a trip is added, removed or renamed — refreshTripLinks. */
    const refreshTripLinks = () => {
      lines.filter((l) => l.category === 'meetings').forEach((l) => {
        const tr = rowOf(l.id);
        const sel: any = tr && $('[data-trip-link-select]', tr);
        if (sel) { sel.options = V.tripLinkOptions(); sel.value = l.linkedTrip ?? ''; }
      });
    };
    /* There is no base PICKER any more. A fringe line's base is the position it
       was generated FROM, so a control offering to repoint it would offer to
       break the one relationship that makes the line exist — and the orphan it
       could create (a fringe line pointing at a position no longer on the
       budget) is now unreachable rather than merely discouraged. */

    /* ── The review layer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The grid owns MARKING (a mark is made on the row it is about); the review
       panel owns the list, the memo and the decision. Both read and write the
       same review object and announce it with fireReview(), so neither ever
       reaches into the other's DOM. */
    let review: any = loadReview();
    const role = () => (document.documentElement.dataset.cbfRole === 'cor' ? 'cor' : 'vendor');
    const markOf = (id: string) => flagFor(review, id);

    const SEV_OPTIONS = [
      { label: severityMeta[SEVERITIES.REQUIRED].label, value: SEVERITIES.REQUIRED },
      { label: severityMeta[SEVERITIES.QUESTION].label, value: SEVERITIES.QUESTION },
    ];
    const REASON_OPTIONS = REASONS.map((r: any) => ({ label: r.label, value: r.key }));

    /** Seed one row's mark editor from the mark it currently carries. */
    const fillMarkForm = (tr: HTMLElement, line: any) => {
      const f = markOf(line.id);
      const sev: any = $('[data-mark-sev-input]', tr);
      if (sev) { sev.options = SEV_OPTIONS; sev.value = f?.severity ?? SEVERITIES.REQUIRED; }
      const reason: any = $('[data-mark-reason-input]', tr);
      if (reason) { reason.options = REASON_OPTIONS; reason.value = f?.reason ?? ''; }
      const note: any = $('[data-mark-note-input]', tr);
      if (note) note.value = f?.note ?? '';
      setText($('[data-mark-form-line]', tr), line.label || 'Untitled line');
    };

    /** The mark as the two roles see it: always to the COR, only once issued to
        the vendor. */
    const paintMark = (tr: HTMLElement | null, line: any) => {
      if (!tr) return;
      const f = markOf(line.id);
      const visible = !!f && (role() === 'cor' || isIssued(review));
      const band = $('[data-mark]', tr);
      show(band, visible);
      if (band) band.dataset.sev = f?.severity ?? '';
      if (f) {
        setText($('[data-mark-reason]', tr), reasonLabel(f.reason));
        setText($('[data-mark-note]', tr), f.note || 'No note written.');
        setText($('[data-mark-by]', tr), `${f.author} · ${f.date}`);
      }
      show($('[data-mark-state="off"]', tr), !f);
      show($('[data-mark-state="on"]', tr), !!f);
      show($('[data-mark-remove]', tr), !!f);
    };

    const paintReview = () => lines.forEach((l) => paintMark(rowOf(l.id), l));

    /** Write the review, tell everyone, repaint the rows. */
    const commitReview = () => {
      saveReview(review);
      paintReview();
      fireReview(review);
    };

    /* ── Pristine row templates, one per SHAPE, captured BEFORE any edit ────────
       Cloning a real, freshly-rendered row of the same shape is how an added line
       gets its markup: the new row's esa-* legos and its colspan collapse are
       byte-identical to the SSR ones, with zero duplicated markup. */
    const rowTemplates: Record<string, HTMLTableRowElement> = {};
    Object.values(SHAPES).forEach((shape: any) => {
      const src = $$(`tr[data-shape="${shape}"]`)[0] as HTMLTableRowElement | undefined;
      if (src) rowTemplates[shape] = src.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
    });
    /* Kept aside rather than assumed present in a shape template: whether a row
       carries a fringe cell is a fact about its CATEGORY, and the templates are
       keyed by shape. fillRow adds or drops it per line. */
    const fringeCellTemplate = ($('.cbf-lct__cell--fringe') as HTMLTableCellElement | null)
      ?.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableCellElement | undefined;

    /* ── Painting: fillRow writes identity + entry VALUES (never during typing),
       paintRow writes everything DERIVED. ── */
    const fillRow = (tr: HTMLElement, line: any) => {
      tr.dataset.line = line.id;
      tr.dataset.shape = line.shape;
      tr.dataset.cat = line.category;
      tr.dataset.block = blockOf(line);

      /* ── Reconcile the cell COUNT before touching any value ────────────────
         Row templates are captured per SHAPE, and shape A is shared by the one
         category that renders a fringe cell and the five that span over it. So a
         cloned row has to gain or lose that cell to match the line it is about
         to hold — every row still totals seven columns, which is what keeps
         Amount lined up with the subtotals and the foot. */
      const rateCell = $('.cbf-lct__cell--rate', tr) as HTMLTableCellElement | null;
      let fringeCell = $('.cbf-lct__cell--fringe', tr) as HTMLTableCellElement | null;
      if (hasFringeCell(line) && !fringeCell && fringeCellTemplate) {
        fringeCell = fringeCellTemplate.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableCellElement;
        rateCell?.after(fringeCell);
      } else if (!hasFringeCell(line) && fringeCell) {
        fringeCell.remove();
      }
      if (rateCell) rateCell.colSpan = rateSpan(line);

      $$('[data-field]', tr).forEach((el: any) => {
        const key = el.dataset.field;
        if (key === 'label') el.value = line.label ?? '';
        else if (key === 'qtyUnit') el.value = line.qtyUnit ?? '';
        else if (key === 'qty2Unit') el.value = line.qty2Unit ?? '';
        else if (key === 'pct') el.value = ((line.pct ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1);
        else if (key === 'fringePct') el.value = fringePctText(line);
        else if (key === 'count') el.value = numText(travelCount(line));
        /* Places, codes and descriptions are not numbers — the generic branch
           below would run them through numText and put NaN in the box. */
        else if (key === 'locality' || key === 'acctCode' || key === 'desc' || key === 'purpose') el.value = line[key] ?? '';
        else if (key === 'staff') el.value = V.staffText(line);
        else if (key === 'partialDays') el.value = numText(line.partialDays ?? 0);
        else el.value = numText(line[key]);
      });
      const gsaSel: any = $('[data-gsa-select]', tr);
      if (gsaSel) { gsaSel.options = gsaOptionsFor(line); gsaSel.value = line.gsaKey ?? ''; }
      /* The three identity pickers. Options are constant lists, so they are safe
         to seed on every fill — only the revealed one is ever reachable. */
      const posCombo: any = $('[data-position-combo]', tr);
      if (posCombo) { posCombo.options = positionOptions(); posCombo.value = line.staffKey ?? ''; }
      const tuSel: any = $('[data-time-unit-select]', tr);
      if (tuSel) { tuSel.options = timeUnitOptions(); tuSel.value = timeUnit(line).key; }
      const tkSel: any = $('[data-travel-kind-select]', tr);
      if (tkSel) { tkSel.options = travelKindOptions(); tkSel.value = line.travelKind ?? ''; }
      const vkSel: any = $('[data-vehicle-kind-select]', tr);
      if (vkSel) { vkSel.options = vehicleKindOptions(); vkSel.value = line.vehicleKind ?? ''; }
      const vtSel: any = $('[data-vehicle-type-select]', tr);
      if (vtSel) { vtSel.options = V.vehicleTypeOptions(); vtSel.value = line.vehicleType ?? ''; }
      const mkSel: any = $('[data-meeting-kind-select]', tr);
      if (mkSel) { mkSel.options = V.meetingKindOptions(); mkSel.value = line.meetingKind ?? ''; }
      const okSel: any = $('[data-other-kind-select]', tr);
      if (okSel) { okSel.options = V.otherKindOptions(); okSel.value = line.otherKind ?? ''; }
      const tlSel: any = $('[data-trip-link-select]', tr);
      if (tlSel) { tlSel.options = V.tripLinkOptions(); tlSel.value = line.linkedTrip ?? ''; }
      const del = $('[data-delete-line] button', tr);
      const name = displayLabel(line) || 'new line';
      del?.setAttribute('aria-label', `Delete line — ${name}`);
      del?.setAttribute('title', `Delete line — ${name}`);
    };

    const paintRow = (tr: HTMLElement | null, line: any, priced: any) => {
      if (!tr) return;
      const a = priced.amounts;
      const sum = allocSum(line);
      const over = gsaOver(line);

      /* Basis — shape C shows its resolved base dollars, which is the whole point:
         the dependency is legible ON the row, not implied by it. */
      const baseCell = $('[data-cell="base"]', tr);
      if (baseCell) {
        setText(baseCell, `${money(baseAmountFor(line, lines, a))} base · ${baseLabelFor(line, lines)}`);
        setText($('[data-cell="fringe-pct"]', tr), fringeRateText(line));
        setText($('[data-cell="fringe-src"]', tr), fringeSrcText(line));
      }

      /* Fringe, on the row that decides it — and, beside it, the dollars it just
         sent to the section below, which is the reason the field is here rather
         than there. */
      if ($('[data-fringe]', tr)) {
        setText($('[data-fringe-note]', tr), fringeAmtText(line, a));
        setText($('[data-fact="fringe"]', tr), fringeFactText(line));
        const ovr = V.fringeOverridden(line);
        show($('[data-fringe-override]', tr), ovr);
        show($('[data-fringe-follows]', tr), !ovr);
        setBtnLabel($('[data-fringe-reset]', tr), V.fringeResetText());
      }

      /* ── The facts a line carries beside its name ──────────────────────────
         Accounting code, vendor-defined badge, the kind pickers that only some
         categories show, and the travel line's own destination and purpose. */
      show($('[data-acct-edit]', tr), !derivedRow(line));
      show($('[data-acct-derived]', tr), derivedRow(line));
      setText($('[data-acct-derived]', tr), acctFactText(line));
      show($('[data-fact="acct"]', tr), !derivedRow(line));
      setText($('[data-fact="acct"]', tr), acctFactText(line));
      show($('[data-vendor-badge]', tr), V.vendorDefined(line));
      show($('[data-kind-pick="meetings"]', tr), line.category === 'meetings');
      show($('[data-kind-pick="other"]', tr), line.category === 'other');
      show($('[data-trip-link]', tr), line.category === 'meetings');
      const tripFact = $('[data-fact="trip-link"]', tr);
      show(tripFact, line.category === 'meetings' && !!line.linkedTrip);
      setText(tripFact, line.linkedTrip && tripOf(line.linkedTrip) ? `Travel: ${tripLabel(tripOf(line.linkedTrip))} trip` : '');
      $$('[data-desc]', tr).forEach((el) => show(el, V.takesDesc(line)));
      show($('[data-vehicle-type]', tr), V.takesVehicleType(line));
      show($('[data-vehicle-type-err]', tr), !!vehicleKind(line.vehicleKind)?.needsType && !line.vehicleType);
      if (line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) {
        const loose = isLooseTravel(line);
        show($('[data-dest-err]', tr), loose && !(line.locality ?? '').trim());
        const pf = $('[data-fact="purpose"]', tr);
        show(pf, loose && !!line.purpose);
        setText(pf, line.purpose ? `Purpose: ${line.purpose}` : '');
        show($('[data-travel-partial]', tr), V.hasPartialDays(line));
      }
      show($('[data-staff]', tr), isSalaryLine(line));
      show($('[data-rate-step]', tr), isSalaryLine(line));

      /* A typed vehicle rate, read against the GSA rate for the same type. */
      const cmpText = V.compareText(line);
      show($('[data-compare]', tr), !!cmpText);
      setText($('[data-compare-text]', tr), cmpText);
      const above = V.compareAbove(line);
      show($('[data-compare-above]', tr), above);
      if (above) setText($('[data-compare-note]', tr), V.gsaErrText(line));

      /* A derived row owns none of its own cells. */
      const derived = derivedRow(line);
      show($('[data-alloc-derived]', tr), derived);
      show($('[data-alloc-editor]', tr), !derived);
      show($('[data-fact="alloc"]', tr), !derived);
      show($('[data-delete-line]', tr), !derived);

      /* Identity — reveal the one control that names a line in THIS category, and
         keep the derived names (fringe, travel) current. */
      const ident = identOf(line);
      $$('[data-ident]', tr).forEach((el) => show(el, el.dataset.ident === ident));
      setText($('[data-cell="ident-derived"]', tr), displayLabel(line));

      /* Quantity — derived rows read as derived and name the line they follow. */
      show($('[data-qty-typed]', tr), !line.qtyFrom);
      show($('[data-qty-derived]', tr), !!line.qtyFrom);
      setText($('[data-cell="derived-qty"]', tr), `${qtyFmt(effectiveQty(line, lines))} ${line.qtyUnit ?? ''}`.trim());
      setText($('[data-cell="derived-src"]', tr), line.qtyFrom ? `Follows ${labelOfId(line.qtyFrom)}` : '');
      /* The way back, offered only to a line that has actually broken a link —
         and naming the source, because "Follow again" alone would be asking the
         vendor to remember which line it was. */
      const refollow = $('[data-qty-refollow]', tr);
      show(refollow, !!line.qtyFromWas && !line.qtyFrom);
      if (line.qtyFromWas && !line.qtyFrom) {
        setText($('[data-refollow-qty]', tr), `Follow ${labelOfId(line.qtyFromWas)}`);
      }
      show($('[data-qty2]', tr), line.qty2 != null);

      /* Three renderings of the unit, one live: open text, a time-unit SELECT on
         a personnel line, or a value where a vehicle kind already fixed it. */
      const locked = unitLocked(line);
      show($('[data-unit-open]', tr), !locked);
      show($('[data-unit-select]', tr), unitPicked(line));
      show($('[data-unit-locked]', tr), unitStatic(line));
      setText($('[data-unit-locked]', tr), lockedUnit(line));

      /* ── Personnel: the position name, and the rate's unit ── */
      const isSal = isSalaryLine(line);
      const named = !!positionKey(titleOfLine(line));
      show($('[data-position-err]', tr), isSal && !named);
      const rateField = $('[data-field="rate"]', tr);
      const rateSuffix = rateSuffixOf(line);
      setSuffix(rateField, rateSuffix);
      /* The suffix takes its width off the input, so the box has to grow with it
         — a time unit changed from months to hours changes both. */
      rateField?.classList.toggle('cbf-lct__field--rate-unit', !!rateSuffix);
      show($('[data-unit2-open]', tr), !locked);
      show($('[data-unit2-locked]', tr), locked);
      setText($('[data-unit2-locked]', tr), lockedUnit2(line));

      /* Travel — the whole chain is repainted, because raising a TRIP's occurrence
         count has to move every line under it at once. */
      if (line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) {
        const k = travelKind(line.travelKind);
        /* The destination is only this line's business while it is on its own —
           joining a trip hands the question back to the head, and leaving one
           hands it here. Dragging a line between the two is what makes this
           show and hide, so it is painted rather than rendered once. */
        const loose = isLooseTravel(line);
        show($('[data-travel-dest]', tr), loose);
        setText($('[data-cell="travel-chain"]', tr), travelChain(line));
        setText($('[data-cell="travel-count-unit"]', tr), k?.countLabel ?? '');
        show($('[data-travel-duration]', tr), !!k?.durationLabel);
        setText($('[data-cell="travel-duration-unit"]', tr), k?.durationLabel ?? '');
        /* The rate cell has exactly two states, and only one of them is ever on
           screen: the vendor's own box, or the claim that replaced it. The claim
           shows the number and the year it belongs to — nothing else. Its
           locality, the sentence about the superseded rate and the two answers
           themselves live in the dialog, which is where they are decided. */
        const claimed = claimsStandard(line);
        show($('[data-typed-rate]', tr), !claimed);
        show($('[data-standard-claim]', tr), claimed);
        $('[data-standard-claim]', tr)?.setAttribute('title', standardSrcText(line));
        show($('[data-standard-toggle]', tr), canClaimStandard(line));
        setBtnLabel($('[data-standard-toggle]', tr), claimed ? 'Use my own rate' : 'Use the standard rate');
        show($('[data-cell="typed-src"]', tr), !claimed);
        setText($('[data-cell="typed-src"]', tr), typedSrcText(line));
        setText($('[data-cell="standard-rate"]', tr), standardAmountText(line));
        setText($('[data-standard-badge] .esa-badge__text', tr), standardBadgeText(line));
        const season = V.seasonText(line);
        show($('[data-standard-season]', tr), !!season);
        setText($('[data-standard-season]', tr), season);
        setText($('[data-fact="travel-rate"]', tr), money(effectiveRate(line)));
        setText($('[data-fact="travel-rate-src"]', tr), claimed ? standardSrcText(line) : typedSrcText(line));
      }

      /* Staffing months against the contract period — shown in both states. */
      const monthsWrap = $('[data-months-wrap]', tr);
      if (monthsWrap) {
        const tested = monthsTested(line);
        show(monthsWrap, tested);
        if (tested) {
          const bad = monthsOver(line);
          show($('[data-months="pass"]', tr), !bad);
          show($('[data-months="fail"]', tr), bad);
          setText($('[data-months-note]', tr), monthsText(line));
          const mErr = $('[data-months-err]', tr);
          show(mErr, bad);
          if (bad) setErrText(mErr, monthsErrText(line));
        }
      }

      /* Rate — shape B is a REFERENCED value, and the gate shows both verdicts. */
      setText($('[data-cell="ref-rate"]', tr), money(effectiveRate(line)));
      setText($('[data-cell="ref-src"]', tr), gsaSrcText(line));
      show($('[data-gsa="pass"]', tr), isScheduleRated(line) && !overridden(line) && !!gsaOf(line));
      show($('[data-gsa="under"]', tr), overridden(line) && !over);
      show($('[data-gsa="over"]', tr), over);
      show($('[data-override-wrap]', tr), overridden(line));
      setBtnLabel($('[data-toggle-override]', tr)?.closest('.esa-button') ?? null,
        overridden(line) ? 'Use the GSA rate' : 'Use my own rate');
      /* Above GSA is a NOTE now — stated, noted for the COR, never a failure. */
      const gErr = $('[data-gsa-err]', tr);
      show(gErr, over);
      if (over) setText(gErr, gsaErrText(line));

      /* The capitalization threshold — where the item filed itself and what that
         does to it, or the override that overruled the sort. */
      const capWrap = $('[data-cap-wrap]', tr);
      if (capWrap) {
        const tested = thresholdTested(line);
        show(capWrap, tested);
        if (tested) {
          const ovr = capOverride(line);
          show($('[data-cap="filed"]', tr), !ovr);
          show($('[data-cap="override"]', tr), ovr);
          setText($('[data-cap-filed]', tr), capFiledText(line, a));
          setText($('[data-cap-why]', tr), capWhyText(line));
          setText($('[data-cap-why-ovr]', tr), capWhyText(line));
          show($('[data-cap-against]', tr), V.capAgainst(line, a));
          if (ovr) setText($('[data-cap-err]', tr), capOverrideText(line, a));
          show($('[data-cap-reset]', tr), !!V.capResetText(line));
        }
      }
      /* The move to the other class — on itemized lines and lumps alike. */
      $$('[data-cap-toggle]', tr).forEach((el) => {
        show(el, onThreshold(line));
        setBtnLabel(el, capToggleText(line));
      });

      /* And the lump the threshold cannot reach into. */
      const capLump = $('[data-cap-lump]', tr);
      if (capLump) {
        const open = capLumpOpen(line, a);
        show(capLump, open);
        if (open) setText($('[data-cap-lump-err]', tr), capLumpText(line, a));
        const filed = $('[data-cap-lump-filed]', tr);
        show(filed, onThreshold(line));
        setText(filed, onThreshold(line) ? capWhyText(line) : '');
      }

      /* Amount — typed only for a lump. */
      setText($('[data-cell="amount"]', tr), usd(a[line.id] ?? 0));

      /* Work element — unfinished must READ unfinished. */
      show($('[data-alloc-state="set"]', tr), sum === 100);
      show($('[data-alloc-state="unset"]', tr), sum !== 100);
      setBtnLabel($('[data-alloc-state="set"]', tr), allocSummary(line), allocTitle(line));
      setBtnLabel($('[data-alloc-state="unset"]', tr), allocSummary(line), allocTitle(line));
      const rErr = $('[data-alloc-row-err]', tr);
      show(rErr, sum !== 100);
      if (sum !== 100) setErrText(rErr, allocErrText(line));

      /* Inline background tint — never a colored left border — and only for the
         states that BLOCK submission. See V.rowFlag. */
      const flag = V.rowFlag(line, lines, a) ?? '';
      if (flag) tr.dataset.flag = flag;
      else tr.removeAttribute('data-flag');

      /* The COR lens reads the same row as VALUES. Painted on every recompute so
         switching lenses mid-edit shows what the vendor actually just typed. */
      setText($('[data-fact="label"]', tr), displayLabel(line) || 'Untitled line');
      setText($('[data-fact="qty"]', tr), qtyFactText(line));
      show($('[data-fact="qty"]', tr), !line.qtyFrom);
      setText($('[data-fact="rate"]', tr), money(line.rate ?? 0));
      setText($('[data-fact="pct"]', tr), `${((line.pct ?? 0) * 100).toFixed(1)}%`);
      setText($('[data-fact="amount"]', tr), usd(a[line.id] ?? 0));
      const allocFact = $('[data-fact="alloc"]', tr);
      setText(allocFact, allocFactText(line));
      allocFact?.setAttribute('title', allocTitle(line));
      /* The inherited split, which both roles read the same way. */
      const allocDerived = $('[data-alloc-derived]', tr);
      setText(allocDerived, allocFactText(line));
      allocDerived?.setAttribute('title', allocTitle(line));
    };

    const paintGroups = (priced: any) => {
      categories.forEach((cat: any) => {
        /* All of them, not the first: Personnel — Fringe reports its subtotal
           twice — once on its own section heading for the COR, and once in the
           foot for the vendor, who cannot see that section. A querySelector here
           would leave whichever came second frozen at its server-rendered value. */
        $$(`[data-cat-total="${cat.key}"]`).forEach((el) =>
          setText(el, usd(priced.byCategory[cat.key] ?? 0)),
        );
        (cat.blocks ?? []).forEach((b: any) => {
          const k = `${cat.key}:${b.key}`;
          setText($(`[data-block-total="${k}"]`), usd(priced.byBlock[k] ?? 0));
        });
      });
      /* Trip heads carry facts of their own — and the COR lens reads them as
         values, because a reviewer does not restructure someone else's itinerary. */
      trips.forEach((t: any) => {
        const head = $(`[data-trip="${t.key}"]`);
        if (!head) return;
        setText($('[data-trip-fact]', head), countOf(t.occurrences, 'trips'));
        setText($('[data-trip-purpose-fact]', head), t.purpose ? `Purpose: ${t.purpose}` : 'No purpose given');
        show($('[data-trip-dest-err]', head), !(t.locality ?? '').trim());
        /* The whole journey, when a meeting is linked to it. */
        const cost = V.tripCostText(t.key, lines, priced.amounts);
        const costEl = $('[data-trip-cost]', head);
        show(costEl, !!cost);
        setText(costEl, cost);
      });
      /* The budget-level settings restate themselves for the COR lens. */
      setText($('[data-fact="fringe-default"]'), `Default fringe ${V.fringeDefaultText()}%`);
      setText($('[data-fringe-default-note]'), V.fringeDefaultNote(lines));
      setText($('[data-fact="threshold"]'), `Capitalization threshold ${usd(contract.capitalizationThreshold)} per item`);
    };

    const paintTotals = (t: any) => {
      setText($('[data-total="direct"]'), usd(t.direct));
      setText($('[data-total="indirectBase"]'), usd(t.indirectBase));
      setText($('[data-total="indirect"]'), usd(t.indirect));
      setText($('[data-total="total"]'), usd(t.total));
      setText($('[data-indirect-rate]'), pct(cfg.rate));
      setText($('[data-indirect-ref]'), cfg.referenceDoc ?? '');
      setText($('[data-indirect-scope]'), scopeText(cfg));
      setText(
        $('[data-total-note]'),
        `${pct(t.pctOfValue, 1)} of the ${usd(contract.contractValue)} Contract Value · ${
          t.remaining >= 0 ? `${usd(t.remaining)} remaining` : `${usd(Math.abs(t.remaining))} over`
        }`,
      );
    };

    /* ── The allocation sheet committed a split ─────────────────────────────
       The grid owns the line, so the sheet states the intent and the grid carries
       it out — the same shape every other sheet on this screen uses. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:save-alloc', (e: any) => {
      const { id, allocations } = e.detail ?? {};
      const line = id ? lineById(id) : null;
      if (!line || !allocations) return;
      line.allocations = { ...allocations };
      const tr = rowOf(line.id);
      if (tr) flash([tr]);
      recompute(line.id);
    });

    /* ── The standard-rate dialog confirmed a claim ─────────────────────────
       Same shape: the dialog states the claim, the grid carries it out.
       normalizeClaim is still the one gate on what a claim may hold — mileage
       has no locality, and a year always resolves — so a claim from the dialog
       is normalized exactly like one made anywhere else. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:save-standard', (e: any) => {
      const { id, standard } = e.detail ?? {};
      const line = id ? lineById(id) : null;
      if (!line || !standard) return;
      line.standard = normalizeClaim(line, standard);
      const tr = rowOf(line.id);
      if (tr) { fillRow(tr, line); flash([tr]); }
      recompute(line.id);
    });

    /** The one broadcast the reconciliation rail and the overhead block consume. */
    const fireTotals = (t: any) => {
      document.dispatchEvent(
        new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:totals', {
          detail: {
            total: t.total,
            direct: t.direct,
            indirect: t.indirect,
            indirectBase: t.indirectBase,
            remaining: t.remaining,
            pctOfValue: t.pctOfValue,
            failing: gsaFailingLines(lines).length,
            unallocated: unallocatedLines(lines).length,
            /* Both directions of the threshold now count — an item filed too high
               is as misfiled as one filed too low. */
            capitalFailing: thresholdMisfiled(lines).length + untestableEquipmentLumps(lines).length,
            monthsFailing: monthsOverPeriod(lines).length,
            /* The full gate and the COR notes, computed from the SAME live lines
               the grid just priced — the rail renders these rather than
               re-deriving a subset of them from counts. */
            checks: submitChecksFor(lines, cfg),
            advisories: advisoriesFor(lines),
            /* Subtotals by the vendor's accounting code, for the code summary. */
            byCode: priceLines(lines, cfg).byCode,
            codeCounts: lines.reduce((acc: any, l: any) => {
              const c = acctCodeOf(l, lines);
              acc[c] = (acc[c] ?? 0) + 1;
              return acc;
            }, {}),
            threshold: contract.capitalizationThreshold,
          },
        }),
      );
    };

    /* The money moment: the rows that just moved because of THIS edit get a brief
       tint. dependentsOf() names exactly those — linked quantities and the
       shape-C lines computed off the edited line's staff group. */
    const flash = (els: (HTMLElement | null)[]) => {
      els.filter(Boolean).forEach((el) => {
        const node = el as HTMLElement;
        node.classList.remove('is-recomputed');
        void node.offsetWidth; // restart the animation on a repeat edit
        node.classList.add('is-recomputed');
        window.setTimeout(() => node.classList.remove('is-recomputed'), 1500);
      });
    };

    /* ── Keep the generated Fringe section in step with the salary section ────
       syncFringeLines() rewrites the fringe LINES; this reconciles the ROWS they
       are shown in. A position that just appeared gets a row cloned from the
       shape-C template (the same device an added line uses), a position whose
       last salary line was deleted loses its row, and everything that survived
       keeps its node — so a repaint never rebuilds a row that did not change.
       Runs before pricing, because the lines have to exist to be priced. */
    let fringeSig = '';
    const syncFringeRows = () => {
      syncFringeLines(lines);
      const tbody = $('tbody[data-cat="personnel-fringe"]');
      if (!tbody) return;
      const want = lines.filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-fringe');
      /* Only the SET matters here. A rate typed on a salary row changes what the
         rows say, not which rows exist — and paintRow already covers what they
         say, so a keystroke must not move nodes around under the reader. */
      const sig = want.map((l) => l.id).join('|');
      if (sig === fringeSig) return;
      fringeSig = sig;

      const wanted = new Set(want.map((l) => l.id));
      $$('tr[data-line]', tbody).forEach((tr) => {
        if (!wanted.has(tr.dataset.line!)) tr.remove();
      });

      const tpl = rowTemplates[SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE];
      want.forEach((line) => {
        let tr = rowOf(line.id);
        if (!tr && tpl) {
          tr = tpl.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
          tbody.appendChild(tr);
          fillRow(tr, line);
          fillMarkForm(tr, line);
          paintMark(tr, line);
          wireRow(tr);
          tr.classList.add('is-added');
          window.setTimeout(() => tr!.classList.remove('is-added'), 1500);
        }
        /* Re-append in list order so the section reads down like the salary
           section it follows. Nothing here is an input, so there is no caret or
           open dropdown to lose. */
        if (tr && tr.parentElement === tbody) tbody.appendChild(tr);
      });
    };

    let tripSig = '';
    const recompute = (originId?: string) => {
      syncFringeRows();
      /* A meeting linked to a trip that no longer exists is linked to nothing —
         and every meeting's trip picker lists the trips there are NOW. Rebuilt
         only when the set of trips or their names changed. */
      lines.forEach((l) => { if (l.linkedTrip && !tripOf(l.linkedTrip)) delete l.linkedTrip; });
      const sig = trips.map((t: any) => `${t.key}=${tripLabel(t)}`).join('|');
      if (sig !== tripSig) { tripSig = sig; refreshTripLinks(); }
      const priced = priceLines(lines, cfg);
      lines.forEach((l) => paintRow(rowOf(l.id), l, priced));
      paintGroups(priced);
      paintTotals(priced.totals);
      fireTotals(priced.totals);
      if (originId) flash(dependentsOf(originId, lines).map((l: any) => rowOf(l.id)));
      return priced;
    };

    /* ── Wiring ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The mark editor lives in an esa-popover, which toggles on any click inside
       its anchor while the slotted panel sits INSIDE that anchor — so a click on
       one of its fields would close the popover mid-edit. Stopping propagation at
       the panel keeps the edit alive (the popover's own outside-click check still
       passes, since the panel is a light-DOM child of the host), and because the
       guard stops the bubble, its Save and Remove are handled HERE rather than by
       the root delegate. Known hub gap, logged in docs/hub-issues.md.

       The allocation editor used to need the same guard. It is a side sheet now,
       so it needs none: it is not anchored to anything. */
    const wireRow = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      $$('[data-mark-panel]', tr).forEach((panel) =>
        panel.addEventListener('click', (ev) => {
          const t = ev.target as HTMLElement;
          if (t.closest('[data-mark-save]')) saveMark(tr);
          else if (t.closest('[data-mark-remove]')) removeMark(tr);
          ev.stopPropagation();
        }),
      );
    };

    /** Write (or rewrite) this row's mark from what the editor holds. */
    const saveMark = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;
      const sev = ($('[data-mark-sev-input]', tr) as any)?.value || SEVERITIES.REQUIRED;
      const reason = ($('[data-mark-reason-input]', tr) as any)?.value || 'other';
      const note = String(($('[data-mark-note-input]', tr) as any)?.value ?? '').trim();

      const flag = {
        lineId: line.id,
        severity: sev === SEVERITIES.QUESTION ? SEVERITIES.QUESTION : SEVERITIES.REQUIRED,
        reason,
        note,
        author: reviewMeta.reviewer,
        date: reviewMeta.today,
      };
      const i = (review.flags ?? []).findIndex((f: any) => f.lineId === line.id);
      if (i >= 0) review.flags[i] = flag;
      else review.flags = [...(review.flags ?? []), flag];
      /* Re-marking a line the vendor already ticked off puts it back on the list —
         the mark changed, so "addressed" is no longer a claim about this mark. */
      review.addressed = (review.addressed ?? []).filter((id: string) => id !== line.id);

      commitReview();
      closeMark(tr);
    };

    const removeMark = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;
      review.flags = (review.flags ?? []).filter((f: any) => f.lineId !== line.id);
      review.addressed = (review.addressed ?? []).filter((id: string) => id !== line.id);
      commitReview();
      fillMarkForm(tr, line);
      closeMark(tr);
    };

    /** esa-popover keeps `open` as a public reflected property. */
    const closeMark = (tr: HTMLElement) => {
      const pop: any = $('[data-mark-popover]', tr);
      if (pop) pop.open = false;
    };

    $$('tr[data-line]').forEach(wireRow);
    lines.forEach((l) => {
      const tr = rowOf(l.id);
      if (tr) fillMarkForm(tr, l);
    });

    /* Selects are property-driven — seed the SSR rows once. There are five of them
       per row now (schedule row, percentage base, position, travel kind, vehicle
       kind), so this runs the same fillRow the added rows use rather than keeping
       a second, drifting list of what needs seeding. */
    lines.forEach((l) => {
      const tr = rowOf(l.id);
      if (tr) fillRow(tr, l);
    });
    /* ── Add-line menus ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Every menu action is `category|block|shape|kind`. What the menu OFFERS is
       the vendor's vocabulary, not the model's: Vehicles lists the four vehicle
       kinds rather than "GSA schedule rate (B)" and "Quantity × rate (A)", and a
       trip lists the six travel kinds. Only where a category genuinely offers a
       structural choice with no better name — Supplies' itemize-or-lump — does
       the shape surface at all, and even then in plain words. */
    const SHAPE_MENU_LABEL: Record<string, string> = {
      [SHAPES.QTY_RATE]: 'Itemized — quantity × cost per unit',
      [SHAPES.AUTHORITY]: 'GSA schedule rate',
      [SHAPES.PCT_OF_BASE]: 'Percentage of a base',
      [SHAPES.LUMP]: 'Lump sum',
      [SHAPES.TRAVEL]: 'Travel line',
    };
    const wireAddMenu = (menu: any) => {
      const cat = categories.find((c: any) => c.key === menu.dataset.addMenu);
      if (!cat) return;
      /* Meetings & training and Other direct costs name a KIND, the way vehicles
         do: the menu is the kinds, and the vendor-defined one comes last. */
      if (cat.kinds) {
        menu.items = cat.kinds.flatMap((k: any) => [
          ...(k.vendorDefined ? [{ divider: true }] : []),
          {
            label: k.vendorDefined ? `${k.label} — describe your own` : k.label,
            action: `${cat.key}||${SHAPES.QTY_RATE}|${k.key}`,
          },
        ]);
        if (cat.key === 'other') {
          menu.items = [...menu.items, { divider: true },
            { label: 'Lump sum — one amount', action: `other||${SHAPES.LUMP}|other` }];
        }
        return;
      }
      if (cat.key === 'vehicles') {
        menu.items = VEHICLE_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({
          label: k.label,
          action: `vehicles||${k.shape}|${k.key}`,
        }));
        return;
      }
      if (cat.key === 'personnel-salary') {
        /* One shape per line, so the only choice worth making is the TYPE —
           naming the shape here would be internal vocabulary. With the split
           off there is no type to choose, so there is one item. */
        if (!personnelSplit.on) {
          menu.items = [{ label: 'Position', action: `personnel-salary|technical|${SHAPES.QTY_RATE}|` }];
          return;
        }
        menu.items = PERSONNEL_TYPES.map((t: any) => ({
          label: `${t.label} position`,
          action: `personnel-salary|${t.key}|${SHAPES.QTY_RATE}|`,
        }));
        return;
      }
      if (cat.entersEquipment) {
        /* The one entry point for equipment, whichever class it ends up in — so
           the menu must NOT ask the question the sort answers. A new line always
           opens in the expensed block (a $0 item is not capital) and re-files
           itself the moment a rate is typed. What is left to choose is the only
           thing that genuinely differs: an ITEM, which has a per-item cost and
           therefore files itself, and a LUMP, which has none and therefore
           cannot — which is why a large one is held at the gate. */
        menu.items = [
          { label: 'Item — a quantity and a cost each',
            action: `${cat.key}|expensed|${SHAPES.QTY_RATE}|` },
          { label: 'Lump sum — one amount, no per-item cost',
            action: `${cat.key}|expensed|${SHAPES.LUMP}|` },
        ];
        return;
      }
      const blocks = cat.blocks ?? [null];
      menu.items = blocks.flatMap((b: any, bi: number) =>
        [
          ...(bi > 0 ? [{ divider: true }] : []),
          ...cat.shapes.map((s: string) => ({
            label: b ? `${b.label} — ${SHAPE_MENU_LABEL[s]}` : SHAPE_MENU_LABEL[s],
            action: `${cat.key}|${b ? b.key : ''}|${s}|`,
          })),
        ],
      );
    };
    $$('[data-add-menu]').forEach(wireAddMenu);

    /** A trip's own add menu: the six travel kinds, scoped to that trip. */
    const wireTravelMenu = (menu: any) => {
      menu.items = TRAVEL_KINDS.map((k: any) => ({
        label: k.label,
        action: `travel|${menu.dataset.addTravel}|${SHAPES.TRAVEL}|${k.key}`,
      }));
    };
    $$('[data-add-travel]').forEach(wireTravelMenu);


    /* ── Add a line INLINE — no modal. A new row is a real, editable row of the
       chosen shape, appended in the right group, focused and ready to type. It
       starts with allocations:{} — correctly unallocated, which moves the gate. */
    const insertRow = (tr: HTMLTableRowElement, catKey: string, block: string) => {
      const tbody = $(`tbody[data-cat="${catKey}"]`);
      if (!tbody) return;
      if (block) {
        const rows = $$(`tr[data-line][data-block="${block}"]`, tbody);
        const anchor = rows[rows.length - 1] ?? $(`tr[data-blockhead="${block}"]`, tbody);
        anchor?.after(tr);
        if (!anchor) tbody.appendChild(tr);
      } else {
        tbody.appendChild(tr);
      }
    };

    const defaultGsaKeyFor = (catKey: string) => {
      const sibling = lines.find((l) => l.category === catKey && l.gsaKey);
      /* Fall back within the category's OWN scope — gsaRates[0] is a travel row,
         which would seed a new vehicles line with a lodging rate. */
      return sibling?.gsaKey ?? gsaRatesFor(catKey)[0]?.key ?? gsaRates[0].key;
    };


    /* ── A claim on a published rate ────────────────────────────────────────
       Two answers, both the vendor's: WHERE the rate is claimed for and WHICH
       fiscal year's schedule is being claimed. The location seeds from wherever
       the line already goes — its trip's destination, or its own — but it is a
       separate value from that moment on, because the destination names the
       journey while this prices the line, and letting one edit both is what made
       a retyped city silently move money in the previous model.

       Mileage carries no location: one published national figure per year. */
    const normalizeClaim = (line: any, ref: any) => {
      const next: any = { year: Number(ref?.year) || defaultRateYear() };
      if (standardNeedsLocality(line.travelKind)) next.locality = ref?.locality ?? travelLocality(line) ?? '';
      /* Seasonal lodging carries its season; nothing else does. */
      if (ref?.season && line.travelKind === 'lodging') next.season = ref.season;
      return next;
    };

    /** Seed the fields a KIND is responsible for. Called on add, and again every
        time the vendor changes the kind — the kind owns the units and the rate
        source, so changing it has to reset both rather than leave a lodging
        line's "nights" sitting on an airfare line. */
    const applyTravelKind = (line: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = travelKind(kindKey);
      line.travelKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      line.duration = k.durationLabel ? (line.duration ?? 1) : undefined;
      /* M&IE counts its first and last travel days apart, at 75% — a new line
         opens on the common case, two of them. Any other kind has none. */
      if (k.partialRate) line.partialDays = line.partialDays ?? 2;
      else delete line.partialDays;
      if (!k.vendorDefined) delete line.desc;
      /* The kind decides WHAT is counted, so switching kind re-seeds the count
         from the last line in this trip counting the same unit — a lodging line
         turned into a mileage line must not keep "2" and silently mean vehicles. */
      line.count = seedCountFor(line.trip, kindKey, lines.filter((l) => l.id !== line.id));
      /* Every travel line has a typed rate; the kind only decides whether a CLAIM
         on a published rate is available at all. Switching to a kind with nothing
         published drops a live claim — a parking estimate cannot claim a lodging
         schedule — and the typed rate underneath is what the line falls back to,
         which is exactly why the claim never discarded it. */
      line.rate = line.rate ?? 0;
      delete line.gsaKey;
      delete line.rateOverride;
      if (!hasStandardRate(kindKey)) delete line.standard;
      else if (line.standard) line.standard = normalizeClaim(line, line.standard);
      syncLabel(line);
    };

    const applyVehicleKind = (line: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = vehicleKind(kindKey);
      line.vehicleKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      line.shape = k.shape;
      line.qtyUnit = k.unit;
      if (k.secondUnit) { line.qty2 = line.qty2 ?? 1; line.qty2Unit = k.secondUnit; }
      else { delete line.qty2; delete line.qty2Unit; }
      if (k.shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY) {
        delete line.rate;
        /* The TYPE looks the row up — a line with no type yet references none,
           and says so, rather than defaulting to some class nobody picked. */
        line.gsaKey = gsaKeyForVehicle(kindKey, line.vehicleType);
      } else {
        delete line.gsaKey;
        delete line.rateOverride;
        line.rate = line.rate ?? 0;
      }
      if (k.vendorDefined) { delete line.vehicleType; line.qtyUnit = line.qtyUnit || ''; }
      else delete line.desc;
      syncLabel(line);
    };

    /** Meetings & training and Other direct costs: the kind fixes the unit(s). */
    const applyCostKind = (line: any, kindKey: string) => {
      const k = line.category === 'meetings' ? meetingKind(kindKey) : otherKind(kindKey);
      if (line.category === 'meetings') line.meetingKind = kindKey;
      else line.otherKind = kindKey;
      if (!k) return;
      if (k.unit) line.qtyUnit = k.unit;
      else if (line.qtyUnit && [...MEETING_KINDS, ...OTHER_KINDS].some((x: any) => x.unit === line.qtyUnit)) line.qtyUnit = '';
      if (k.secondUnit) { line.qty2 = line.qty2 ?? 1; line.qty2Unit = k.secondUnit; }
      else { delete line.qty2; delete line.qty2Unit; }
    };

    const addLine = (catKey: string, block: string, shape: string, kind = '', seed: any = null) => {
      const line: any = {
        id: `new-${++seq}-${Date.now().toString(36)}`,
        category: catKey,
        shape,
        label: '',
        allocations: {},
      };
      if (block) line.block = block;
      if (shape === SHAPES.QTY_RATE) { line.qty = 1; line.qtyUnit = ''; line.rate = 0; }
      if (shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY) { line.qty = 1; line.qtyUnit = ''; line.gsaKey = defaultGsaKeyFor(catKey); }
      /* Where the CATEGORY fixes the unit the row renders it as a value, so the
         line has to carry it — an empty qtyUnit under a locked cell would read
         correctly and export blank. */
      const fixedUnit = categoryFixedUnit(catKey);
      if (fixedUnit) line.qtyUnit = fixedUnit;
      /* Shape C is never ADDED — every one of them is generated by
         syncFringeLines from a salary line, so there is no seeding to do here. */
      if (shape === SHAPES.LUMP) line.amount = 0;
      if (catKey === 'personnel-salary') {
        /* Untitled on purpose. There is no roster to pick a default off, and
           guessing a title would put a position on the budget that nobody chose.
           The row opens with its combobox empty and an error saying so. */
        line.label = '';
        line.staffKey = '';
        line.block = block === 'admin' ? 'admin' : 'technical';
        line.qty = 1;
        line.staff = 1;
        line.timeUnit = 'month';
        line.qtyUnit = timeUnit(line).plural;
        /* No fringePct: a new line FOLLOWS the budget's default fringe rate, so a
           change to the default reaches it. Typing a rate on the line is what
           makes it an override. */
      }
      if (shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL) {
        /* The ungrouped block is a block, not a trip — it files the line and
           leaves `trip` null, which is what every travel derivation reads to
           decide whether there is a journey behind this cost. */
        line.block = block || NO_TRIP;
        line.trip = block && block !== NO_TRIP ? block : null;
        if (!line.trip) line.locality = '';
        applyTravelKind(line, kind || TRAVEL_KINDS[0].key);
      }
      /* An imported line arrives with its values; they land BEFORE the kind is
         applied, so the kind can still fix the units it owns. */
      if (seed) Object.assign(line, seed);
      if (catKey === 'vehicles' && kind) applyVehicleKind(line, kind);
      if ((catKey === 'meetings' || catKey === 'other') && kind) applyCostKind(line, kind);
      syncLabel(line);

      const tpl = rowTemplates[line.shape];
      if (!tpl) return;
      const tr = tpl.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
      insertRow(tr, catKey, line.block ?? block);
      lines.push(line);
      fillRow(tr, line);
      /* A cloned row inherits the template row's mark markup — reset it, because
         a brand-new line carries no mark of its own. */
      fillMarkForm(tr, line);
      paintMark(tr, line);
      wireRow(tr);
      tr.classList.add('is-added');
      window.setTimeout(() => tr.classList.remove('is-added'), 1500);
      /* A batch import recomputes once at the end, and does not scroll the page
         to every row it lands. */
      if (seed?.__quiet) { delete line.__quiet; return tr; }
      recompute();
      fileLine(line);
      tr.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', behavior: 'smooth' });
      window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        /* Focus whatever this category's FIRST question actually is — the free-text
           description on most rows, but the duration on a travel line, whose kind
           and locality the vendor already answered by choosing the menu item. */
        const first: any =
          line.shape === SHAPES.TRAVEL
            ? /* A line with no trip has one question a line inside a trip has
                 already been answered for it: where it goes. */
              (isLooseTravel(line) ? $('[data-field="locality"]', tr) : null)
                ?? $('[data-field="duration"]', tr)
                ?? $('[data-field="count"]', tr)
            : $('[data-field="label"]', tr);
        first?.shadowRoot?.querySelector('input')?.focus();
      });
      return tr;
    };

    /* ── A row that changes SHAPE has to be rebuilt ──────────────────────────
       Two edits do this: switching a vehicle kind between a schedule-rated lease
       and a typed premium, and moving a misfiled item across the capitalization
       threshold. Both mean the row's cells no longer exist in the same
       arrangement, so the honest move is to re-clone from the template for the
       new shape rather than to hide and reveal cells that were never there. */
    const rebuildRow = (line: any, catKey?: string, block?: string) => {
      const old = rowOf(line.id);
      const tpl = rowTemplates[line.shape];
      if (!old || !tpl) return;
      const tr = tpl.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
      old.remove();
      insertRow(tr, catKey ?? line.category, block ?? blockOf(line));
      fillRow(tr, line);
      fillMarkForm(tr, line);
      paintMark(tr, line);
      wireRow(tr);
      recompute(line.id);
      flash([tr]);
      return tr;
    };

    /* ── Filing an equipment line into the class its cost puts it in ─────────
       Runs where the numbers change, right after the recompute that produced the
       new per-item cost. The row moves between the two sub-blocks of ONE section
       — Supplies and Capital Equipment sit under the same heading, the way
       Technical and Administrative do — so this is a re-seat within a tbody
       rather than a jump between sections. It still rebuilds, because insertRow
       is what knows where a block's rows end, and it still flashes and scrolls:
       a row relocating under you must be visible even when it only moved a
       little way.

       Re-entrancy guard: rebuildRow recomputes, and a recompute must not be able
       to start a second sort underneath the first. */
    let filing = false;
    const fileLine = (line: any) => {
      if (filing || !onThreshold(line)) return;
      const move = autoFileEquipment(line, priceLines(lines, cfg).amounts);
      if (!move) return;
      filing = true;
      const tr = rebuildRow(line, line.category, blockOf(line));
      filing = false;
      /* Both block subtotals changed, and so did the indirect base — the rate
         reaches the expensed block and not the capital one. */
      recompute();
      tr?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', behavior: 'smooth' });
    };

    /* ── Adding a TRIP ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       A travel line with no trip has no locality, no travelers and no occurrence
       count to stand on, so Travel's category-level add makes the container, not
       a line. The head is cloned from a real trip head for the same reason rows
       are: the esa-* legos and the sub-block subtotal wiring come with it. */
    const tripHeadTemplate = ($('tr[data-trip]:not([data-trip=""])') as HTMLTableRowElement | null)
      ?.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement | undefined;

    /** The head row for a trip that already exists in the model, unattached. */
    const buildTripHead = (trip: any): HTMLTableRowElement | null => {
      if (!tripHeadTemplate) return null;
      const head = tripHeadTemplate.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
      head.dataset.blockhead = trip.key;
      head.dataset.trip = trip.key;
      setText($('.cbf-lct__block-label', head), tripLabel(trip));
      setText($('[data-trip-cell="window"]', head), trip.window);
      const totalCell = $('[data-block-total]', head);
      totalCell?.setAttribute('data-block-total', `travel:${trip.key}`);
      setText(totalCell, usd(0));
      const dest: any = $('[data-trip-field="locality"]', head);
      if (dest) dest.value = trip.locality ?? '';
      const occ: any = $('[data-trip-field="occurrences"]', head);
      if (occ) occ.value = String(trip.occurrences ?? 1);
      const menu: any = $('[data-add-travel]', head);
      if (menu) { menu.dataset.addTravel = trip.key; wireTravelMenu(menu); }
      return head;
    };

    /** Put the caret where the trip's first unanswered question is. */
    const focusTripHead = (head: HTMLElement) => {
      window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        const dest: any = $('[data-trip-field="locality"]', head);
        dest?.shadowRoot?.querySelector('input')?.focus();
      });
    };

    const addTrip = () => {
      /* createTrip registers the trip in BOTH lists — the pricing engine's and
         Travel's block list, which paintGroups walks for sub-block subtotals. */
      const trip: any = createTrip();
      const head = buildTripHead(trip);
      if (!head) return;
      $(`tbody[data-cat="travel"]`)?.appendChild(head);
      head.classList.add('is-added');
      window.setTimeout(() => head.classList.remove('is-added'), 1500);
      head.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', behavior: 'smooth' });
      recompute();
      focusTripHead(head);
      return head;
    };

    /* ── Deleting a TRIP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       The mirror of adding one. A trip is the container its lines stand on —
       locality, travelers, occurrences all live on the head — so a line cannot
       outlive it, and "delete the trip, keep the lines" would leave rows with
       no destination and no multiplier. The lines go with it.

       The confirmation is not ceremony: the number of lines and what they cost
       is exactly the fact that decides whether the vendor meant this, and it is
       the one thing a trash icon on a collapsed head cannot show. An empty trip
       skips it — there is nothing to lose. */
    const dropTrip = (key: string) => {
      lines.filter((l) => l.trip === key).forEach((l) => {
        rowOf(l.id)?.remove();
        const i = lines.findIndex((x) => x.id === l.id);
        if (i >= 0) lines.splice(i, 1);
      });
      $(`tr[data-trip="${key}"]`)?.remove();
      removeTrip(key);
      recompute();
    };

    /* ── The other way to delete a trip: keep what it was carrying ──────────
       The container goes, the costs stay. Each line is refiled into the
       ungrouped block by the SAME refile() the drag uses, so it carries away the
       destination it was pricing at and re-points nothing — the one thing that
       does change is `occurrences`, which was a fact about the journey: a line
       that is no longer part of one happens once. That is a real change to the
       money, and it is why this sits behind the same dialog as the destructive
       option rather than being a quiet second trash icon. */
    const ungroupTrip = (key: string) => {
      const head = $(`tr[data-trip="${key}"]`);
      const freed = lines.filter((l) => l.trip === key);
      const looseHead = $(`tr[data-blockhead="${NO_TRIP}"]`);
      freed.forEach((l) => {
        const tr = rowOf(l.id);
        if (!tr) return;
        /* refile() takes the ROW as well as the line — it re-fills the cells the
           move changes (the destination box that just became this line's own,
           and the schedule row behind it). */
        refile(l, tr, NO_TRIP);
        /* Appended to the end of the ungrouped block, which is where the block's
           own Add button puts a line — and, in the list, after the last line
           already there, so the array and the DOM keep agreeing. */
        const siblings = $$(`tr[data-line][data-block="${NO_TRIP}"]`).filter((r) => r !== tr);
        const last = siblings[siblings.length - 1];
        if (last) last.after(tr);
        else if (looseHead) looseHead.after(tr);
        if (last?.dataset.line) moveInList(lines, l.id, last.dataset.line, 'after');
      });
      head?.remove();
      removeTrip(key);
      recompute();
      flash(freed.map((l) => rowOf(l.id)));
    };

    const delDialog: any = document.querySelector('[data-del-trip-dialog]');
    let tripPendingDelete = '';

    const askDeleteTrip = (key: string) => {
      const trip = trips.find((t: any) => t.key === key);
      const inTrip = lines.filter((l) => l.trip === key);
      if (!inTrip.length) { dropTrip(key); return; }
      if (!delDialog) { dropTrip(key); return; }
      const priced = priceLines(lines, cfg);
      const cost = inTrip.reduce((sum, l) => sum + (priced.amounts[l.id] ?? 0), 0);
      tripPendingDelete = key;
      /* Says what each button DOES, in the order they sit in the footer — the
         count and the money are the facts that decide which one is meant. */
      const occ = trip?.occurrences ?? 1;
      setText(
        $('[data-del-trip-body]', delDialog),
        `${tripLabel(trip)} has ${countOf(inTrip.length, 'lines')} worth ${usd(cost)}. `
        + 'Keep the lines and they move to “Not part of a trip” with this destination'
        + (occ > 1
          ? `, each priced once instead of ${countOf(occ, 'times')}. `
          : '. ')
        + 'Delete them and they come off the budget.',
      );
      delDialog.show();
    };

    delDialog?.addEventListener('click', (e: any) => {
      const t = e.target as HTMLElement;
      if (t.closest('[data-del-trip-cancel]')) { tripPendingDelete = ''; delDialog.close(); return; }
      if (t.closest('[data-del-trip-keep]')) {
        if (tripPendingDelete) ungroupTrip(tripPendingDelete);
        tripPendingDelete = '';
        delDialog.close();
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-del-trip-confirm]')) {
        if (tripPendingDelete) dropTrip(tripPendingDelete);
        tripPendingDelete = '';
        delDialog.close();
      }
    });

    /* ── Reordering, and the one gesture that is Travel's alone ───────────────
       Three outcomes, all reached with the same grab (see src/lib/row-reorder.ts
       for the zones and the keyboard equivalents):

         · dropped between two rows in the same sub-block → the order changes
         · dropped between two rows in a DIFFERENT sub-block, or onto a
           sub-block head → the line is refiled there. In Supplies that is
           Office ⇄ Field; in Travel it is one trip to another, and the line's
           schedule rate re-points at the new destination on the way, because a
           lodging line that moved to Boise and kept Portland's ceiling is the
           quiet kind of wrong a reviewer catches months later.
         · dropped onto the MIDDLE of another travel line → the two become a new
           trip, opened with the caret in its destination field.

       The COR lens does not get any of it: a reviewer marks lines, they do not
       restructure someone else's budget (see the role rules at the end of this
       script — the handle is hidden there for the same reason delete is).

       Order lives in the `lines` array, not in the DOM. Every price, subtotal
       and repaint walks that array, and syncFringeRows re-appends a whole
       section from it — so a DOM-only move would survive exactly one keystroke.
       moveInList() keeps the two agreeing. */
    const catOfRow = (tr: HTMLElement) => tr.dataset.cat ?? '';
    const blockOfRow = (el: HTMLElement) => el.dataset.block ?? el.dataset.blockhead ?? '';
    /* A sub-block head carries no category of its own — its tbody does. */
    const catOfEl = (el: HTMLElement) =>
      el.dataset.cat ?? (el.closest('tbody[data-cat]') as HTMLElement | null)?.dataset.cat ?? '';

    /* Fringe is generated and re-appended in list order on every recompute, so
       there is nothing to hold on to; Capital Equipment is a destination its
       lines are FILED into, and its order follows Supplies. Everything else is
       the vendor's to arrange. */
    const REORDERABLE = (catKey: string) =>
      catKey !== 'personnel-fringe';

    /** Refile a line under a different sub-block of the same category. */
    const refile = (line: any, tr: HTMLElement, blockKey: string) => {
      if ((line.block ?? '') === blockKey) return;
      line.block = blockKey || undefined;
      if (line.category === 'travel') {
        /* ── Grouping and ungrouping are the same gesture ──────────────────
           Dropping into the ungrouped block takes the line OUT of its trip, and
           the destination has to survive the move or the line silently re-prices
           at CONUS: it carries away a copy of the trip's destination as its own.
           The mirror holds too — joining a trip drops the line's own copy,
           because the head is then the only place that destination is typed and
           two of them would drift. */
        const toTrip = blockKey && blockKey !== NO_TRIP ? blockKey : null;
        if (toTrip) delete line.locality;
        else line.locality = travelLocality(line);
        line.trip = toTrip;
        line.block = blockKey || NO_TRIP;
        /* Nothing re-prices on a move any more. A claim on a published rate
           names its own location and year, and those are answers the vendor gave
           about the RATE, not about which trip the line is filed under — so
           dragging a line between trips changes what it is grouped with and
           nothing else. That is the whole point of having taken pricing off the
           destination. */
        syncLabel(line);
      }
      fillRow(tr, line);
    };

    /** Where a row's record belongs in `lines`, expressed against a neighbour. */
    const reseat = (line: any, targetRow: HTMLElement, where: 'before' | 'after') => {
      const refId = targetRow.dataset.line;
      if (refId) moveInList(lines, line.id, refId, where);
    };

    /**
     * Two travel lines become one trip. The new trip inherits the facts of the
     * trip the vendor dropped ONTO — that row is the one staying put, so its
     * destination and window are the ones on screen at the moment of the
     * gesture, and starting the vendor from blank would have thrown away the
     * answer they already gave. What it does NOT inherit is a name they have
     * confirmed, which is why the caret lands in the destination field.
     */
    const groupIntoTrip = (line: any, tr: HTMLElement, target: any, targetRow: HTMLElement) => {
      const from = tripOf(line.trip);
      const onto = tripOf(target.trip);
      const trip: any = createTrip({
        /* An ungrouped target has no trip to inherit from, but it does have the
           one fact that matters — its own destination — so the new trip opens
           on the place the vendor already named rather than on a blank. */
        locality: onto?.locality ?? target.locality ?? '',
        window: onto?.window ?? 'Dates to be set',
        occurrences: onto?.occurrences ?? 1,
      });
      const head = buildTripHead(trip);
      if (!head) return false;

      /* The new block opens directly BELOW the trip the target came out of, so
         the two lines stay where the vendor was looking instead of jumping to
         the end of Travel. Below it and not in place of it: a trip's rows are
         siblings in one tbody with nothing but their head to bound them, so
         inserting the head where the target sat would strand every row after it
         under a heading it does not belong to. */
      const ontoRows = onto
        ? $$(`tr[data-line][data-block="${onto.key}"]`).filter((r) => r !== targetRow)
        : [];
      const anchor = ontoRows[ontoRows.length - 1]
        ?? (onto ? $(`tr[data-trip="${onto.key}"]`) : null)
        ?? targetRow;
      /* A trip promoted out of two UNGROUPED lines cannot open where they sat:
         the ungrouped block is the last thing in Travel, so a head inserted
         after one of its rows would sit under the "Not part of a trip" heading
         and claim the rows below it. It opens immediately above that heading
         instead — last of the trips, which is what it now is. */
      const looseHead = $(`tr[data-blockhead="${NO_TRIP}"]`);
      if (!onto && looseHead) looseHead.before(head);
      else anchor.after(head);
      head.after(targetRow);
      targetRow.after(tr);
      refile(target, targetRow, trip.key);
      refile(line, tr, trip.key);
      reseat(line, targetRow, 'after');

      /* Whichever trips that emptied are no longer facts about this budget. */
      emptiedTrips(lines, [from?.key, onto?.key]).forEach((key) => {
        $(`tr[data-trip="${key}"]`)?.remove();
        removeTrip(key);
      });

      head.classList.add('is-added');
      window.setTimeout(() => head.classList.remove('is-added'), 1500);
      recompute(line.id);
      focusTripHead(head);
      return true;
    };

    initRowReorder({
      root,
      itemSelector: 'tr[data-line]',
      landmarkSelector: 'tr[data-blockhead]',
      /* A line exchanges places with lines in its own CATEGORY — not its own
         sub-block, which is what lets it move between trips — and with nothing
         at all once the COR lens is on. */
      scopeOf: (el) => {
        if (role() === 'cor') return null;
        const cat = catOfEl(el);
        if (!cat || !REORDERABLE(cat)) return null;
        if (el.dataset.line && derivedRow(lineById(el.dataset.line))) return null;
        return cat;
      },
      /* Travel only, and never with a line already in the same trip — that
         drop is a reorder wearing a group's clothes, and it would leave the
         vendor naming a trip identical to the one they started in. */
      canGroup: (a, b) => {
        if (catOfRow(a) !== 'travel' || catOfRow(b) !== 'travel') return false;
        /* Two lines that are both ungrouped are the case this gesture is BEST
           at — "these two are actually one trip" — even though they share a
           block. The rule it is really enforcing is that a line already in a
           trip cannot be regrouped into the trip it is already in. */
        const [ba, bb] = [blockOfRow(a), blockOfRow(b)];
        return ba !== bb || (ba === NO_TRIP && bb === NO_TRIP);
      },
      /* A drop target is either a line or a whole sub-block, and the
         announcement has to name whichever it actually was. */
      describe: (el) => {
        if (el.dataset.trip) return `the ${tripLabel(tripOf(el.dataset.trip))} trip`;
        if (el.dataset.blockhead) return `${el.dataset.blockhead}`;
        const line = lineById(el.dataset.line ?? '');
        return line ? displayLabel(line) || 'this line' : 'this line';
      },
      onDrop: ({ item, target, zone }) => {
        const line = lineById(item.dataset.line ?? '');
        if (!line) return false;

        if (zone === 'into' && target.dataset.line) {
          const other = lineById(target.dataset.line);
          return other ? groupIntoTrip(line, item, other, target) : false;
        }

        /* Dropping on a sub-block HEAD means "put it in here", with no opinion
           about where among the lines already there — so it lands last, the
           same place the block's own Add button puts a new line. */
        if (zone === 'into') {
          const blockKey = target.dataset.blockhead ?? '';
          const siblings = $$(`tr[data-line][data-block="${blockKey}"]`).filter((r) => r !== item);
          const last = siblings[siblings.length - 1];
          const fromTrip = line.trip;
          if (last) last.after(item); else target.after(item);
          refile(line, item, blockKey);
          if (last?.dataset.line) moveInList(lines, line.id, last.dataset.line, 'after');
          emptiedTrips(lines, [fromTrip]).forEach((key) => {
            $(`tr[data-trip="${key}"]`)?.remove();
            removeTrip(key);
          });
          recompute(line.id);
          return true;
        }

        const fromTrip = line.trip;
        if (zone === 'before') target.before(item); else target.after(item);
        reseat(line, target, zone);
        refile(line, item, blockOfRow(target));
        emptiedTrips(lines, [fromTrip]).forEach((key) => {
          $(`tr[data-trip="${key}"]`)?.remove();
          removeTrip(key);
        });
        recompute(line.id);
        return true;
      },
    });

    root.addEventListener('menu-action', (e: any) => {
      const [catKey, block, shape, kind] = String(e.detail ?? '').split('|');
      if (catKey && shape) addLine(catKey, block, shape, kind ?? '');
    });

    /* ── Every esa-* control fires a composed `change`; delegate the lot ── */
    /* ── Typing in a position box ────────────────────────────────────────────
       esa-combobox filters its own options and will only COMMIT one of them, so
       the create affordance has to be an option. Each debounced search re-seeds
       that row's options with a "Use <term>" row when the term matches no title
       already on this budget. */
    root.addEventListener('search', (e: any) => {
      const el = e.target as HTMLElement;
      if (!el?.dataset || !('positionCombo' in el.dataset)) return;
      (el as any).options = positionOptionsFor(e.detail?.term ?? '');
    });

    root.addEventListener('change', (e: any) => {
      const el = e.target as HTMLElement;

      /* A TRIP field belongs to the block head, not to any one row — and editing
         it moves every line under that trip at once, which is the entire reason
         travel is grouped this way. The trip objects are looked up by key inside
         the pricing engine, so they are updated in place rather than copied. */
      const head = el.closest?.('tr[data-trip]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (head && (el.dataset as any).tripField) {
        const trip: any = tripOf(head.dataset.trip!);
        if (!trip) return;
        const field = (el.dataset as any).tripField;
        const raw = (el as any).value;

        if (field === 'purpose') {
          /* Why the trip happens. Prices nothing — it is read, by the COR. */
          trip.purpose = String(raw ?? '').trim();
          recompute();
          return;
        }
        if (field === 'locality') {
          /* Renames the trip and every line under it, and moves NOT ONE DOLLAR.
             This used to re-point each line's schedule row, which meant a
             corrected spelling could change the budget — the reason pricing came
             off the destination in the first place. A line that claims a
             published rate states its own location when it makes the claim. */
          trip.locality = String(raw ?? '').trim();
            } else {
          /* A journey happens at least once. */
          trip[field] = Math.max(1, num(raw));
        }

        setText($('.cbf-lct__block-label', head), tripLabel(trip));
        const moved = lines.filter((l) => l.trip === trip.key);
        moved.forEach(syncLabel);
        recompute();
        /* A renamed trip is renamed in every meeting's trip picker too. */
        refreshTripLinks();
        flash(moved.map((l) => rowOf(l.id)));
        return;
      }

      /* ── Budget-level settings on the section heads ───────────────────────── */
      const budgetField = (el.dataset as any)?.budgetField;
      if (budgetField === 'fringeDefault') {
        /* The default fringe rate. Every salary line that follows it re-prices
           on this keystroke, and so does its generated fringe line; a line that
           overrides it does not move — that is what overriding means. */
        const v = num((el as any).value);
        fringeDefaults.pct = Math.max(0, v) / 100;
        lines.filter((l) => isSalaryLine(l) && !isFringeOverride(l)).forEach((l) => {
          const f: any = $('[data-field="fringePct"]', rowOf(l.id) ?? root);
          if (f) f.value = fringePctText(l);
        });
        recompute();
        flash(lines.filter((l) => l.category === 'personnel-fringe').map((l) => rowOf(l.id)));
        return;
      }
      if (budgetField === 'threshold') {
        /* The contract's capitalization threshold. It re-files the lines the
           sort still owns; a line the vendor placed by hand stays where they put
           it, and is reported if the new threshold disagrees. */
        contract.capitalizationThreshold = Math.max(0, num((el as any).value));
        lines.filter((l) => thresholdTested(l) && !isClassOverride(l)).forEach((l) => fileLine(l));
        recompute();
        document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:threshold', {
          detail: { threshold: contract.capitalizationThreshold },
        }));
        return;
      }
      if ('splitToggle' in ((el.dataset as any) ?? {})) {
        /* Admin/technical is a per-budget choice. Off, the sub-block heads go and
           every position reads under one heading; each line keeps its type, so
           turning it back on restores the split exactly. */
        personnelSplit.on = !!(e.detail?.checked ?? (el as any).checked);
        const tbody = $('tbody[data-cat="personnel-salary"]');
        if (tbody) tbody.dataset.split = personnelSplit.on ? 'on' : 'off';
        const menu: any = $('[data-add-menu="personnel-salary"]');
        if (menu) wireAddMenu(menu);
        return;
      }

      const tr = el.closest?.('tr[data-line]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (!tr) return;
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;
      const d = el.dataset;

      /* ── The identity pickers ── */
      if ('positionCombo' in d) {
        const picked = String((el as any).value ?? '');
        /* Two ways in, one outcome: pick a title this budget already defines, or
           commit the term typed into the box. Either way the line ends up
           carrying the TITLE, and the key is derived from it — so a reused title
           lands on the same position without the vendor having to know that a
           key exists at all. */
        const title = picked.startsWith(NEW_POS)
          ? picked.slice(NEW_POS.length).trim()
          : (rosterFor(lines).find((r: any) => r.key === picked)?.title ?? '');
        line.label = title;
        line.staffKey = positionKey(title);
        /* The line keeps the sub-block it sits in — the block IS the type, and a
           position's type is a fact about the position, not about its title.

           The Fringe section follows on its own: re-titling a line moves it to a
           different position, so one generated fringe line loses its last salary
           line and another gains one. recompute() runs syncFringeLines before it
           prices, so nothing here has to know that. */
        recompute(line.id);
        /* A new title changes the roster every OTHER personnel combobox offers,
           so they all have to be re-seeded — otherwise the second technician's
           box would not list the title the first one just created. */
        refreshPositionOptions();
        return;
      }
      if ('timeUnitSelect' in d) {
        /* The unit decides what the number means and what the rate is per — both
           captions repaint, and neither number is silently reinterpreted. */
        line.timeUnit = (el as any).value;
        line.qtyUnit = timeUnit(line).plural;
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('meetingKindSelect' in d || 'otherKindSelect' in d) {
        applyCostKind(line, (el as any).value);
        fillRow(tr, line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('tripLinkSelect' in d) {
        /* Linking costs nothing and moves nothing — it puts the meeting on the
           trip head's full-cost line, and takes it off the one it left. */
        const v = String((el as any).value ?? '');
        if (v) line.linkedTrip = v; else delete line.linkedTrip;
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('vehicleTypeSelect' in d) {
        line.vehicleType = String((el as any).value ?? '') || undefined;
        /* The type is the GSA lookup — re-point the row it references. */
        if (line.shape === SHAPES.AUTHORITY) line.gsaKey = gsaKeyForVehicle(line.vehicleKind, line.vehicleType);
        syncLabel(line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('travelKindSelect' in d) {
        applyTravelKind(line, (el as any).value);
        fillRow(tr, line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
      if ('vehicleKindSelect' in d) {
        const before = line.shape;
        applyVehicleKind(line, (el as any).value);
        /* Lease → insurance is a change of SHAPE, not just of values. */
        if (line.shape !== before) rebuildRow(line);
        else { fillRow(tr, line); recompute(line.id); }
        return;
      }

      /* The two answers a claim is made OF are not typed on the row any more —
         they are confirmed in cbf-lib-standard-rate-dialog and arrive back on
         'cbf-lib:save-standard'. Neither touches the trip's destination: that
         names the journey, the claim prices the line. */

      if (d.field === 'locality') {
        /* The ungrouped line's own destination — the head's locality handler,
           for an audience of one line. Same three consequences: the schedule row
           it references, the name it goes by, and the sentence saying which of
           the two rate schedules the typed place landed on. */
        /* Names the line. It no longer moves any money: a claim on a published
           rate carries its own location, stated once when the claim is made. */
        line.locality = String((el as any).value ?? '').trim();
        syncLabel(line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }

      if (d.field === 'label') {
        line.label = (el as any).value ?? '';
      } else if (d.field === 'acctCode' || d.field === 'purpose') {
        /* Text, not numbers. The code is the join key to the vendor's ledger;
           the purpose is read by the COR. Neither prices anything. */
        line[d.field] = String((el as any).value ?? '').trim();
      } else if (d.field === 'desc') {
        /* A vendor-defined line's own words for what it is — and its name. */
        line.desc = String((el as any).value ?? '').trim();
        syncLabel(line);
      } else if (d.field === 'staff') {
        /* At least one person; whole people. */
        line.staff = Math.max(1, Math.round(num((el as any).value) || 1));
      } else if (d.field === 'qtyUnit' || d.field === 'qty2Unit') {
        line[d.field] = (el as any).value ?? '';
      } else if (d.field === 'fringePct') {
        /* The one number fringe actually asks for. It lands on the SALARY line;
           the fringe line that follows is regenerated by the next recompute, so
           there is no second place holding a rate that could disagree. */
        line.fringePct = num((el as any).value) / 100;
      } else if (d.field) {
        line[d.field] = num((el as any).value);
      } else if ('gsaSelect' in d) {
        line.gsaKey = (el as any).value;
      } else {
        return;
      }
      recompute(line.id);
      /* Quantity and rate are what the threshold divides, so an equipment line
         re-files itself on the same edit that changed them. */
      fileLine(line);
    });

    /* ── Clicks: add, delete, second quantity, rate override, subcontract seam ── */
    root.addEventListener('click', (e: any) => {
      const t = e.target as HTMLElement;

      if (t.closest?.('[data-add-trip]')) {
        addTrip();
        return;
      }

      if (t.closest?.('[data-open-import]')) {
        document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:open-import'));
        return;
      }

      if (t.closest?.('[data-delete-trip]')) {
        const head = t.closest('tr[data-trip]') as HTMLElement | null;
        if (head?.dataset.trip) askDeleteTrip(head.dataset.trip);
        return;
      }

      const add = t.closest?.('[data-add-line]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (add) {
        const [catKey, block, shape, kind] = String(add.dataset.addLine).split('|');
        addLine(catKey, block, shape, kind ?? '');
        return;
      }

      const sheet = t.closest?.('[data-open-sheet]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (sheet) {
        document.dispatchEvent(
          new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:open-subcontract', {
            detail: { sheetId: sheet.dataset.openSheet, lineId: sheet.dataset.openSheetLine },
          }),
        );
        return;
      }

      const tr = t.closest?.('tr[data-line]') as HTMLElement | null;
      if (!tr) return;
      const line = lineById(tr.dataset.line!);
      if (!line) return;

      if (t.closest('[data-delete-line]')) {
        /* A derived row has no delete affordance, and must not acquire one by
           accident: deleting it would only have it regenerated on the next
           recompute, which reads as a control that does not work. */
        if (derivedRow(line)) return;
        const i = lines.findIndex((l) => l.id === line.id);
        if (i >= 0) lines.splice(i, 1);
        tr.remove();
        recompute();
        return;
      }

      /* ── Breaking and remaking a followed quantity ────────────────────────
         `qtyFrom` is a DEFAULT, not a fact about the line, so both directions
         are the vendor's to take. Detaching freezes the number the link had
         resolved to — the quantity must not blank or jump on the way out — and
         remembers the source in `qtyFromWas` so the link can be remade by name.
         A fringe row is excluded: its quantity is not followed, it is generated,
         and there is nothing underneath to fall back to. */
      if (t.closest('[data-detach-qty]')) {
        if (derivedRow(line) || !line.qtyFrom) return;
        line.qty = effectiveQty(line, lines);
        line.qtyFromWas = line.qtyFrom;
        delete line.qtyFrom;
        fillRow(tr, line);
        recompute();
        return;
      }
      if (t.closest('[data-refollow-qty]')) {
        if (!line.qtyFromWas || !lineById(line.qtyFromWas)) return;
        line.qtyFrom = line.qtyFromWas;
        delete line.qtyFromWas;
        fillRow(tr, line);
        recompute();
        return;
      }

      /* The split is entered on the side sheet — this button is the way in, and
         the summary on it is the way out. */
      if (t.closest('[data-alloc-editor]')) {
        if (derivedRow(line)) return;
        document.dispatchEvent(
          new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:open-alloc', {
            /* The sheet names the line it is splitting, and the grid is what
               knows the name: travel and fringe lines resolve theirs rather
               than carrying it. The amount is priced, so it travels too. */
            detail: {
              line,
              label: displayLabel(line) || 'Untitled line',
              amount: priceLines(lines, cfg).amounts[line.id],
            },
          }),
        );
        return;
      }

      /* ── Claiming a published rate, and dropping the claim ─────────────────
         Making the claim is a confirmation, not an edit: the dialog seeds both
         answers — the location from wherever the line goes, the year from the
         contract's own — states what they resolve to, and hands the claim back
         on 'cbf-lib:save-standard'. Nothing is written here, so opening it and
         looking at the number costs the line nothing.

         Dropping the claim IS an edit, and it stays on the row: it restores the
         typed rate, which was never thrown away, and there is nothing to
         confirm. */
      if (t.closest('[data-standard-edit]') || t.closest('[data-standard-toggle]')) {
        const dropping = !!line.standard && !t.closest('[data-standard-edit]');
        if (dropping) {
          delete line.standard;
          fillRow(tr, line);
          recompute(line.id);
          window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
            ($('[data-typed-rate]', tr) as any)?.shadowRoot?.querySelector('input')?.focus();
          });
          return;
        }
        document.dispatchEvent(
          new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:open-standard', {
            /* The dialog names the line it is pricing, and the grid is what
               knows the name — a travel line resolves its own from its kind and
               its chain rather than carrying one. */
            detail: { line, label: displayLabel(line) || 'Untitled line' },
          }),
        );
        return;
      }

      if (t.closest('[data-toggle-override]')) {
        if (line.rateOverride == null) line.rateOverride = gsaRate(line.gsaKey)?.rate ?? 0;
        else delete line.rateOverride;
        const f: any = $('[data-field="rateOverride"]', tr);
        if (f) f.value = numText(line.rateOverride);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }

      /* ── Moving an equipment line to the other class, by hand ─────────────
         Either direction, itemized or lump. The move PINS the line — the sort
         leaves it where the vendor put it from then on — and if that is against
         the threshold, it is noted for the COR, not refused. */
      if (t.closest('[data-cap-toggle]')) {
        if (!onThreshold(line)) return;
        const to = thresholdTargetFor(line);
        line.classOverride = true;
        line.block = to;
        const moved = rebuildRow(line, line.category, to);
        recompute();
        moved?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'nearest', behavior: 'smooth' });
        return;
      }
      /* Handing a hand-placed item back to the threshold, which re-files it. */
      if (t.closest('[data-cap-reset]')) {
        delete line.classOverride;
        fileLine(line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }

      /* ── A second rate for the same position ─────────────────────────────
         The FY crossover / COLA / step case: a copy of the line, same title and
         split, inserted right below it. The time is divided between the two so
         the position's total time is unchanged, and the caret lands on the new
         line's rate — the one number that is different. */
      if (t.closest('[data-rate-step]')) {
        if (!isSalaryLine(line)) return;
        const total = line.qty ?? 0;
        const first = Math.round((total / 2) * 2) / 2;
        line.qty = first;
        const step: any = {
          ...line,
          id: `new-${++seq}-${Date.now().toString(36)}`,
          qty: Math.max(0, Math.round((total - first) * 100) / 100),
          allocations: { ...(line.allocations ?? {}) },
        };
        delete step.flags;
        const tpl = rowTemplates[SHAPES.QTY_RATE];
        if (!tpl) return;
        const nr = tpl.cloneNode(true) as HTMLTableRowElement;
        tr.after(nr);
        const at = lines.findIndex((l) => l.id === line.id);
        lines.splice(at + 1, 0, step);
        fillRow(tr, line);
        fillRow(nr, step);
        fillMarkForm(nr, step);
        paintMark(nr, step);
        wireRow(nr);
        recompute(line.id);
        flash([tr, nr]);
        window.requestAnimationFrame(() => {
          ($('[data-field="rate"]', nr) as any)?.shadowRoot?.querySelector('input')?.focus();
        });
        return;
      }

      /* Back to the budget's default fringe rate. */
      if (t.closest('[data-fringe-reset]')) {
        delete line.fringePct;
        fillRow(tr, line);
        recompute(line.id);
        return;
      }
    });

    /* ── The overhead block owns the rate AND its scope; this grid owns the base.
       Re-scoping must be visible HERE, so the indirect rows flash on receipt. ── */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:indirect-config', (e: any) => {
      const incoming = e.detail?.config;
      if (!incoming) return;
      cfg = { ...cfg, ...incoming, appliesTo: [...(incoming.appliesTo ?? cfg.appliesTo)] };
      recompute();
      flash($$('[data-indirect-row]'));
    });

    /* ── The review round trip ─────────────────────────────────────────────
       The panel owns the list and the decision; when either changes, the rows
       have to agree. Identity check first: this grid fires the same event, and
       repainting its own echo would be work for nothing. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:review', (e: any) => {
      const incoming = e.detail?.review;
      if (!incoming || incoming === review) return;
      review = incoming;
      lines.forEach((l) => {
        const tr = rowOf(l.id);
        if (tr) fillMarkForm(tr, l);
      });
      paintReview();
    });

    /* Switching lenses changes WHO is reading, which changes whether a mark is
       visible at all — so the marks repaint, and the read-only mirrors are
       already current from the last recompute. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-role:change', paintReview);

    /* "Go to line" from the review panel: bring the row into view and flash it,
       and — for the COR's Edit — open that row's mark editor, so the note is
       always written next to the numbers it is about. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:focus-line', (e: any) => {
      const { lineId, openFlag } = e.detail ?? {};
      const tr = lineId ? rowOf(lineId) : null;
      if (!tr) return;
      tr.scrollIntoView({ block: 'center', behavior: 'smooth' });
      flash([tr]);
      if (openFlag && role() === 'cor') {
        const pop: any = $('[data-mark-popover]', tr);
        if (pop) window.setTimeout(() => { pop.open = true; }, 250);
      }
    });

    /* ── Viewing the budget by the vendor's accounting code ──────────────────
       The code summary (cbf-lib-code-summary) owns the choice; the grid owns the
       rows. Filtering hides lines booked to other codes, and the summary keeps
       the subtotals — so "what does 5300 add up to" is answerable both as a
       number and as the lines that make it. `null` shows everything again. */
    let codeFilter: string | null = null;
    const paintCodeFilter = () => {
      lines.forEach((l) => {
        const tr = rowOf(l.id);
        if (tr) tr.hidden = codeFilter != null && acctCodeOf(l, lines) !== codeFilter;
      });
      root.toggleAttribute('data-code-filtered', codeFilter != null);
    };
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:code-filter', (e: any) => {
      const c = e.detail?.code;
      codeFilter = c == null ? null : String(c);
      paintCodeFilter();
    });

    /* ── Lines arriving from an Excel import ─────────────────────────────────
       The import dialog parses and checks; the grid owns the lines, so the
       dialog hands over plain line records and they are added the same way a
       vendor's own "Add line" adds one — through addLine, kinds and all — then
       priced once. Every gate runs on them like any other line. */
    document.addEventListener('cbf-lib:import-lines', (e: any) => {
      const incoming: any[] = e.detail?.lines ?? [];
      const added: HTMLElement[] = [];
      incoming.forEach((rec) => {
        const { category, block = '', shape, kind = '', ...seed } = rec;
        const tr = addLine(category, block, shape, kind, { ...seed, __quiet: true });
        if (tr) added.push(tr);
      });
      recompute();
      lines.forEach((l) => fileLine(l));
      refreshPositionOptions();
      flash(added);
      added[0]?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'center', behavior: 'smooth' });
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('cbf-lib:imported', { detail: { count: added.length } }));
    });

    /* ── The demo's "Clear all entries" ──────────────────────────────────────
       The seeded budget is SERVER-RENDERED, so a cleared grid is reached by
       wiping it on mount (src/data/lib-demo.mjs says why the state is a flag and
       a reload). Placed here on purpose: after the row templates were captured —
       an added line still clones a real row, so the emptied grid stays fully
       usable — and before the first recompute, so the totals that go out to the
       rail are the empty ones and no seeded number is ever broadcast.

       The trips go with the lines. A trip is the container its lines stand on,
       and a head with nothing under it is not "cleared", it is a leftover. */
    if (isBlank()) {
      trips.slice().forEach((t: any) => {
        $(`tr[data-trip="${t.key}"]`)?.remove();
        removeTrip(t.key);
      });
      /* Every row the server rendered, not just the ones still in `lines` — a
         derived fringe row is generated FROM the salary lines, so it has to go
         with them rather than be left behind pointing at a position that no
         longer exists. */
      $$('tr[data-line]').forEach((tr) => tr.remove());
      lines.length = 0;
      /* The overhead block clears itself and re-broadcasts, but it does so from
         its own mount — this keeps the grid from pricing one recompute against
         an approved rate that is no longer on the screen. */
      cfg = blankConfig();
    }

    /* Mount: paint everything, broadcast once, and re-broadcast on load so a
       sibling whose script initialises after this one still gets the state (the
       event is a pure snapshot, so repeating it is harmless). */
    paintReview();
    recompute();
    const rebroadcast = () => fireTotals(priceLines(lines, cfg).totals);
    if (document.readyState === 'complete') window.setTimeout(rebroadcast, 0);
    else window.addEventListener('load', rebroadcast, { once: true });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* Dense entry surface: 14px table base, 13px for the quiet second lines — and
     DARKENED as it shrinks (text-secondary, never a muted gray). The literal
     sizes are deliberate: the --type-size-* clamps bottom out at 11–12px on
     narrow viewports, which is under the 13px absolute floor. */
  .cbf-lct__lede {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__sr {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* overflow stays VISIBLE: the per-row allocation popover and the add-line shape
     menu position absolutely and cannot escape a clipping ancestor. So the table
     is sized to FIT its column instead of scrolling inside it — the page drops the
     reconciliation rail below the grid before the grid would ever need to scroll
     (--sidebar-content-min on the page). Horizontal scroll is the last resort at
     genuinely narrow widths, where a clipped popover is the lesser evil. */
  .cbf-lct__scroll {
    overflow: visible;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    border-radius: var(--radius-200);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-raised);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cbf-lct__scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  }

  .cbf-lct__table {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 900px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default);
  }

  /* ── Per-category column captions ──
     Quieter than the old single table head, and deliberately so: it repeats nine
     times, and a full-weight header band at every category would read as nine
     tables stacked instead of one budget. It sits BELOW its category heading and
     above that category's rows, so it reads as a caption of the section it
     introduces rather than as a divider between sections. */
  .cbf-lct__colhead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--spacing-150) var(--spacing-300);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-raised);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .cbf-lct__colhead .cbf-lct__col-num { text-align: right; }
  .cbf-lct__col-span { min-width: 380px; }
  /* Column floors sum to ~1020px so the grid fits its column beside the rail on a
     laptop rather than overflowing it. */
  /* Six columns. The old "Rate source" column was dropped: what governs a rate
     (the GSA schedule row, the percentage base, the capitalization test) now
     lives in the Rate cell beside the number it produces, which is why Rate is
     the widest of the entry columns. Floors sum to ~970px — Quantity has to
     hold a number AND the time-unit select side by side, and the line column
     has to hold the longest position title without clipping its last glyph. */
  .cbf-lct__col-line { min-width: 250px; }
  /* Wide enough for the WHOLE multiplication on one line, in every category
     that has one: [12] [months] × [3] [vehicles] is two 76px numbers, two 96px
     unit boxes and the × between them, which measures ~374px. Travel's version
     is narrower (its units are words, not boxes) and a single-quantity line
     narrower still — one width has to hold the widest, because a column cannot
     size itself per row. Anything above this and the cluster wraps, which is
     exactly the stacked reading the chain exists to prevent. */
  .cbf-lct__col-qty { min-width: 388px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-rate { min-width: 215px; }
  /* Holds a rate and the dollars that rate produces, side by side, so it is
     wider than the percentage alone would need. It still exists to be read DOWN
     — "why is this one 22.3%" — but the dollars beside it keep the row readable
     ACROSS, straight into the Amount column next door. Only Personnel — Salary
     fills it; every other section spans its rate cell over the gap. */
  .cbf-lct__col-fringe { min-width: 205px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-we { min-width: 140px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-act { width: 44px; }
  .cbf-lct__col-num { text-align: right; min-width: 115px; }

  .cbf-lct__cell {
    padding: var(--spacing-250) var(--spacing-300);
    vertical-align: top;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-subtle);
  }
  .cbf-lct__cell--num {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .cbf-lct__cell--act { text-align: right; }

  /* ── The grab column inside the line cell ──
     A flex pair, not a seventh table column: a column would have cost every
     category a caption for something that is not a value, and would have made
     the grid wider by the handle's width in nine places instead of one. The
     handle is top-aligned with the identity control rather than centred on the
     row, because a row with two lines of validation under it is still grabbed
     by its first line. */
  .cbf-lct__grab {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: var(--spacing-100);
    /* Reclaim most of the cell's own inline padding so the handle sits in the
       gutter and the description field keeps its column position. */
    margin-inline-start: calc(var(--spacing-200) * -1);
  }
  .cbf-lct__grab > .stack { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
  /* A derived row has no handle, and its identity must still line up with the
     rows above it — so the space the handle would occupy is held, not removed. */
  /* A reserved gutter, not a control — the hub retired the form-height ramp, and
     28px is the width the handle beside it occupies. */
  .cbf-lct__grab-void { width: 28px; flex: none; }
  /* A shape whose cells genuinely don't exist collapses into one spanned cell —
     never a row of dead inputs. */
  .cbf-lct__cell--void { color: var(--color-content-default-secondary); }

  /* ── Category + sub-block header rows ── */
  .cbf-lct__cathead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--spacing-300);
    background: var(--cbf-surface-section-head, var(--color-background-elevation-sunken));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-regular);
  }
  .cbf-lct__cathead .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--cbf-surface-section-head, var(--color-background-elevation-sunken));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-strong);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .cbf-lct__cat-label {
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-content-default);
  }
  .cbf-lct__blockhead th {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-300) var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-500);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-regular);
  }
  .cbf-lct__blockhead .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default);
    vertical-align: middle;
  }
  .cbf-lct__block-label {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-content-default);
  }

  /* ── Quiet second lines ── */
  .cbf-lct__note {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
  /* Where an item filed itself. A statement of fact the vendor did not type, so
     it reads a step above the quiet notes around it — 14px and primary text —
     without becoming a status colour. Filing correctly is the ordinary outcome
     and must not look like an alarm; the consequence line under it stays quiet. */
  .cbf-lct__filed {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }
  /* "Nothing governs this rate" — a typed-rate line. Aligned with the controls in
     its sibling cells rather than floating at the cell's very top. */
  .cbf-lct__void-mark {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    /* Centres the mark against a control-height row; the form-height ramp is gone. */
    line-height: 40px;
  }
  /* A value the vendor did NOT type — referenced or derived. Reads as a value,
     not as an input, and never as a disabled control. */
  .cbf-lct__ref,
  .cbf-lct__derived {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-content-default);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .cbf-lct__derived { font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-regular); }
  /* The count and the duration are one product, so they sit on one line and the
     × stays between them. align-items:center keeps the × and both unit words on
     the boxes' centre line rather than their baselines. */
  .cbf-lct__qty-chain {
    --align: center;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .cbf-lct__times {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    align-self: center;
  }

  /* ── Entry controls — one purpose per control, all md so a row lines up ──
     Fields keep the legos' default chrome (bordered, on surface). An earlier pass
     made them borderless-until-hover to quiet the grid; that buried the affordance
     — a vendor could not see where they were allowed to type until they clicked.
     Discoverability wins over visual quiet on an entry surface. Don't re-quiet
     these without solving the "where do I type" problem some other way. */
  .cbf-lct__field--label { width: 100%; }
  .cbf-lct__field--qty { width: 76px; }
  /* 88px, not 96: two of these plus two numbers and the × have to fit one line
     in the widest case (a supplies item with a second quantity, where BOTH units
     are open text boxes). At 96 the pair overflowed the column by 8px and the
     chain wrapped — which is the stacked reading it exists to prevent. The
     longest house unit word, "months", still fits whole. */
  .cbf-lct__field--unit { width: 88px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--rate { width: 116px; }
  /* Fits "$ 4,750 /month" whole: both affixes are segmented addons that eat into
     the box, and the longest unit word is the one that has to fit. */
  .cbf-lct__field--rate-unit { width: 196px; }
  /* Wide enough for a 4-character rate plus the % suffix chip — at 92px a
     trailing 3 clipped while a trailing 1 fit, which reads as a data error. */
  .cbf-lct__field--pct { width: 104px; }
  .cbf-lct__field--amount { width: 130px; margin-inline-start: auto; }
  .cbf-lct__field--trip { width: 116px; }
  /* 218, not 230: on a travel line outside a trip this field (and the purpose
     field under it) is the widest thing in the line column, and at 230 it pushed
     the table's min-content to ~1455px — past the 1444px floor the page's
     sidebar is measured against, so the grid painted under the rail. */
  .cbf-lct__field--dest { width: 218px; }
  /* A trip's purpose is a sentence, and the head spans the whole entry block. */
  .cbf-lct__field--purpose { width: min(100%, 520px); }
  /* Wide enough that its label sits on one line. */
  .cbf-lct__field--threshold { width: 190px; }
  /* A whole-person count is one or two digits. */
  .cbf-lct__field--staff { width: 64px; }
  /* The vendor's ledger code — short, and quieter than the name above it. */
  .cbf-lct__field--code { width: 180px; }
  .cbf-lct__select { width: 100%; }
  /* The time unit sits beside the number it qualifies, so it is sized like the
     unit box it replaced rather than filling the cell. */
  /* Fits the longest unit word ('months') beside its caret — narrower clips it. */
  .cbf-lct__select--unit { width: 104px; }

  /* A unit a KIND already decided is a value, not a control — it reads in line
     with the field it qualifies rather than as another empty box beside it. */
  .cbf-lct__unit {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    align-self: center;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The dollars a fringe rate generates, beside the rate. It never wraps under
     the field — wrapping is exactly the stacked reading this cell was flattened
     to escape — so the cell holds its row on one line. */
  .cbf-lct__cell--fringe [data-fringe] { flex-wrap: nowrap; }
  .cbf-lct__fringe-amt { white-space: nowrap; }

  /* ── Money ── */
  .cbf-lct__money {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold);
  }

  /* Until esa-popover upgrades it is an unknown element, so EVERY child renders
     inline — including slot="content". Without this guard each row shows its
     whole six-field allocation editor (and, in the COR lens, the mark form)
     stacked in the cell until hydration, which on a grid this size is a long and
     very loud flash. Same :not(:defined) guard the spoke uses on esa-tab-layout.
     If the script ever fails to load, this keeps the panels closed rather than
     dumping every editor onto the page. */
  esa-popover:not(:defined) [slot='content'] { display: none; }

  /* ── Allocation popover panel ── */
  .cbf-lct__alloc { width: 380px; }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-head {
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold);
    color: var(--color-content-default);
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--spacing-300);
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-label {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-input { width: 104px; flex: none; }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-sum {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--spacing-150);
    margin: 0;
    padding-block-start: var(--spacing-200);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-subtle);
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__alloc-sum strong {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--color-content-default);
  }

  /* ── Row state: an inline background tint, NEVER a colored left border ── */
  tr[data-flag='gsa'] > .cbf-lct__cell,
  tr[data-flag='months'] > .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--color-background-utility-danger-subtle);
  }
  /* The capitalization flag is no longer an ERROR. Arithmetic files every item,
     so what is left to mark is a vendor's override and a lump too large to test
     — both "this needs agreement", not "this is wrong". Warning, like the
     allocation flag, and matching the badges the row itself carries. */
  tr[data-flag='capital'] > .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--color-background-utility-warning-subtle);
  }
  tr[data-flag='alloc'] > .cbf-lct__cell {
    background: var(--color-background-utility-warning-subtle);
  }

  /* ── The money moment: rows that just recomputed because of an edit ──────────
     dependentsOf() names them exactly — a salary edit visibly moving its fringe
     line and the cell-phone line whose months follow it. */
  tr.is-recomputed > .cbf-lct__cell,
  tr.is-recomputed > th,
  tr.is-added > .cbf-lct__cell {
    animation: cbf-lct-flash 1.5s var(--easing-enter) 1;
  }
  /* The flash LAYERS over the cell (inset shadow) rather than replacing its
     background-color — otherwise a flagged row loses its danger/warning tint for
     the duration of the flash and then snaps back to it. */
  @keyframes cbf-lct-flash {
    from { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax var(--color-background-utility-info-subtle); }
    to { box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax transparent; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    tr.is-recomputed > .cbf-lct__cell,
    tr.is-recomputed > th,
    tr.is-added > .cbf-lct__cell {
      animation: none;
      box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 100vmax var(--color-background-utility-info-subtle);
    }
  }

  /* ── Totals block ── */
  .cbf-lct__totals th {
    text-align: right;
    padding: var(--spacing-250) var(--spacing-300);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-subtle);
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-content-default);
  }
  .cbf-lct__totals th .cbf-lct__note {
    display: block;
    max-width: none;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-regular);
  }
  .cbf-lct__totals .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-subtle);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .cbf-lct__totals tr:first-child th,
  .cbf-lct__totals tr:first-child .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-strong);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken);
  }
  .cbf-lct__grand th,
  .cbf-lct__grand .cbf-lct__cell {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-default-strong);
    background: var(--color-background-elevation-sunken);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-semibold);
  }
  .cbf-lct__grand .cbf-lct__cell strong { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }

  /* ── The two lenses ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     VENDOR sees entry controls and no mark editor. COR sees the same grid as
     values plus the mark editor, because a reviewer does not type into someone
     else's budget. Both renderings are in the markup; the document's role
     attribute reveals one, so switching lenses never loses an edit. */
  .cbf-lct__fact {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .cbf-lct__fact--label { font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-medium); }

  /* Vendor lens (the default): the mirrors and the mark editor stay out. */
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) .cbf-lct__fact,
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) [data-trip-fact],
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) [data-mark-cell] {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* ── Personnel — Fringe is a REVIEWER's section ────────────────────────────
     The vendor has no business in it: they cannot type into a single cell of it,
     and every dollar it holds is already stated on the salary row that generated
     it, in the Fringe column, next to the rate they typed. Leaving it up gave
     them a whole section of read-only rows to scroll past and to mistake for
     something they had forgotten to fill in.

     It stays in the markup, and stays priced, because it is still the object
     class the COR reviews, the funder reads, and `indirectConfig.appliesTo`
     scopes the overhead rate by. Only the vendor's view of it goes. */
  :global(html:not([data-cbf-role='cor'])) tbody[data-cat='personnel-fringe'] {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* The COR reads the Fringe section itself, so the foot does not repeat it. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-fringe-rollup] {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* COR lens: every entry affordance goes, and the values take their place. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__field,
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__select,
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-toggle-override],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-alloc-state],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-delete-line],
  /* Order is authorship. A reviewer marks lines and disputes numbers; they do
     not rearrange someone else's budget, and they certainly do not decide which
     costs are the same journey. (scopeOf() refuses the gesture in this lens too
     — this only takes the offer off the screen.) */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-drag-handle],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-add-line],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-add-trip],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-delete-trip],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-add-travel],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-trip-controls],
  /* The whole destination block, not just its input: what is left underneath is
     a sentence telling a vendor which schedule their typed place resolved to,
     and the reviewer already reads that resolution in the line's NAME and in the
     schedule row the Rate cell states. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-travel-dest],
  /* The threshold override is the vendor's exception to take, not the COR's. The
     COR still READS where each item filed itself, and the override state on the
     line that took one — that is exactly what they are here to review. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-cap-toggle],
  /* A fringe row's name is already carried by the read-only mirror; leaving the
     derived identity in as well printed it twice. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-ident='derived'],
  /* Breaking or remaking a followed quantity is the VENDOR's call, both ways.
     The COR keeps the derived reading and the "follows …" note underneath it —
     which is the part they review — and neither button. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-qty-detach],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-qty-refollow],
  /* Travel's rate cell goes whole in this lens. The claim is a VENDOR gesture —
     making it, changing it, dropping it — and the reviewer's reading of the same
     line is already printed by the two mirrors below it: the effective rate, and
     the sentence naming the locality and fiscal year it was claimed against.
     Leaving the claim block up printed the rate twice and offered a reviewer a
     pencil into someone else's budget. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-standard-claim],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-standard-toggle],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-cell='typed-src'],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-add-menu] {
    display: none !important;
  }
  /* A shape-C row's percentage cell keeps its resolved base line; only the input
     is replaced. Same for the GSA rows, which already state their source. The
     second-quantity group goes whole, so its × never strands — and so does the
     travel row's count/duration group, whose resolved chain is already printed
     above it as a value. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__cell--qty [data-qty-typed],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__cell--qty [data-travel-count],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__cell--qty [data-travel-duration],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__cell--qty [data-qty2] {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* ── The COR's mark, on the line it disputes ──────────────────────────────
     A tinted comment band INSIDE the cost-line cell — the same discipline the
     rest of this grid follows: status is carried by a badge and a tint, never by
     a colored left border. */
  .cbf-lct__mark {
    margin-block-start: var(--spacing-150);
    padding: var(--spacing-200) var(--spacing-250);
    border-radius: var(--radius-100);
    background: var(--color-background-utility-danger-subtle);
  }
  .cbf-lct__mark[data-sev='question'] { background: var(--color-background-utility-warning-subtle); }
  .cbf-lct__mark [data-mark-sev] { display: none; }
  .cbf-lct__mark[data-sev='required'] [data-mark-sev='required'],
  .cbf-lct__mark[data-sev='question'] [data-mark-sev='question'] { display: inline-flex; }
  .cbf-lct__mark-reason {
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: var(--typography-font-weight-medium);
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary);
  }
  .cbf-lct__mark-note {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--color-content-default);
    max-width: 46ch;
  }

  /* ── The mark editor ── */
  .cbf-lct__markform { width: 360px; }

  /* ── Controls only the vendor operates ─────────────────────────────────────
     Moving an item across the threshold, adding a rate step, resetting a fringe
     override, the admin/technical switch, importing a workbook: all authorship.
     The COR reads where each line landed and what it carries; these go. The
     staff and partial-day groups go whole so their × and + never strand — the
     quantity mirror already states both. */
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-vendor-only],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__cell--qty [data-staff],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) .cbf-lct__cell--qty [data-travel-partial],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-vehicle-type-err],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-dest-err],
  :global(html[data-cbf-role='cor']) [data-trip-dest-err] {
    display: none !important;
  }

  /* ── Personnel with the admin/technical split switched off ───────────────
     The sub-block heads and their subtotals go; the lines stay where they are
     and keep their type, so switching it back on restores the split exactly. */
  tbody[data-split='off'] > tr.cbf-lct__blockhead { display: none; }

  /* Layout primitives set display:flex, which would beat the UA [hidden] rule. */
  [hidden] { display: none !important; }
</style>
